WEBVTT

00:25.650 --> 00:26.190
It.

00:57.620 --> 01:01.420
Hi, everyone. Good evening. Welcome to the Sustainability,

01:01.500 --> 01:04.890
Infrastructure and Development committee meeting on April 16,

01:05.077 --> 01:08.796
2025. The purpose of this meeting is to receive

01:08.908 --> 01:12.252
public input on the GRT business plan.

01:12.436 --> 01:15.756
I will now call on Chris Ferriso, the committee clerk,

01:15.788 --> 01:18.894
to conduct the roll call. Thank you, Chair.

01:18.982 --> 01:22.770
Regional Chair Redmond. Present. Councillor Craig.

01:24.070 --> 01:25.410
Councillor Erb?

01:27.590 --> 01:30.510
I believe he's online. Councilor Foxton.

01:30.590 --> 01:33.518
Present. Councillor Harris. Present.

01:33.654 --> 01:35.010
Councilor Huneck.

01:37.430 --> 01:40.574
Chair James. Present. We have regrets from Councillor

01:40.622 --> 01:44.696
Liggett, Councillor McCabe. Councilor Novak.

01:44.888 --> 01:47.688
Present. Councilor Rodriguez.

01:47.784 --> 01:50.856
Present. Councilor Salonen. Present.

01:51.008 --> 01:54.616
Councillor Schontz. Present. Councillor Verbanovich,

01:54.808 --> 01:58.376
Councilor Williams and Councilor Wolf.

01:58.488 --> 02:01.500
Present. Thank you, Chair. Okay. And we have quorum.

02:02.720 --> 02:06.488
Okay, thank you. We will begin with our land acknowledgment.

02:06.584 --> 02:09.256
We are on the traditional territory of the Anishinaabe,

02:09.288 --> 02:13.032
Chenanton and Haudenosaunee peoples and recognize their

02:13.136 --> 02:16.964
endearing presence on the land. We honor the land, the water that sustains

02:17.012 --> 02:21.044
us, the air we breathe, and Mother Earth, who provides for all living beings.

02:21.172 --> 02:25.156
As we move on these lands, may we do so with respect.

02:25.308 --> 02:28.516
Recognizing the deep cultural and spiritual connections

02:28.628 --> 02:32.720
to Indigenous peoples and those that and their connection to this region.

02:33.020 --> 02:35.240
Let us continue to commit to learning,

02:36.380 --> 02:40.308
to support future generations. Are there

02:40.444 --> 02:43.600
any declarations of pecuniary interest?

02:44.210 --> 02:47.722
Seeing none, we will now proceed with the delegations

02:47.786 --> 02:51.482
as listed on the agenda. I would like to remind all delegations of some

02:51.506 --> 02:54.938
of the guidelines for participation. For those who are joining in

02:54.994 --> 02:58.746
person, please proceed to the podium. When you're

02:58.778 --> 03:02.202
called upon to speak, please press the button on the podium to

03:02.226 --> 03:05.802
turn on the microphone. If it's green, that means it's on.

03:05.986 --> 03:09.786
For those who are joining us electronically, please leave your video camera

03:09.818 --> 03:13.434
turned off and your microphone audio muted until you are called upon

03:13.482 --> 03:16.646
to speak. You will have five. Five minutes. For anyone

03:16.678 --> 03:20.758
who registered after the deadline, you will have three minutes.

03:20.934 --> 03:24.614
If you conduct yourself in any of the following manners, it will result in immediate

03:24.662 --> 03:28.886
removal from the meeting, interrupting counselors, staff or other delegations,

03:28.998 --> 03:33.222
derogatory statements about councillors, staff or others turning

03:33.286 --> 03:37.222
your camera on at inappropriate times, and any hate speech or defamatory

03:37.286 --> 03:41.350
comments. I will now call on the first speaker,

03:41.430 --> 03:45.352
Devin Sisek, for Barrier Free Cambridge. Welcome,

03:45.416 --> 03:48.860
Devin, and you have five minutes.

03:50.720 --> 03:54.344
All right. Good evening, chair, councillors and staff. My name is Devin Sisak,

03:54.392 --> 03:58.072
and I'm speaking tonight on behalf of Barrier Free Cambridge, an accessibility advocacy

03:58.136 --> 04:01.592
group committed to building a more inclusive and accessible city for everyone.

04:01.776 --> 04:05.304
And we're encouraged by the scope and ambition of the GRT business plan,

04:05.392 --> 04:07.976
especially the goals of making transit more intuitive,

04:08.088 --> 04:11.190
equitable and responsive to the needs of riders,

04:11.360 --> 04:15.226
expanding service, growing the overnight network and improving township access

04:15.378 --> 04:19.082
all represent meaningful steps forward. But if we want to deliver on

04:19.106 --> 04:22.346
the plan's promise to put customers at the center,

04:22.458 --> 04:26.266
we must confront a barrier that exists before a single trip

04:26.298 --> 04:30.310
is even taken. And that's the Mobility plus application process.

04:31.170 --> 04:34.602
Firstly, the ableist language and structural barriers that undermined

04:34.666 --> 04:38.724
access and dignity. Currently, Mobility plus applications

04:38.882 --> 04:42.160
ask the questions such as do you pose a safety risk to yourself or

04:42.200 --> 04:45.376
others? Can you be left unsupervised at your destination?

04:45.568 --> 04:48.180
Has anyone told you not to travel alone?

04:49.080 --> 04:52.544
These questions frame disabled people as risks,

04:52.592 --> 04:56.020
liabilities or burdens that must be evaluated and contained.

04:56.440 --> 05:00.432
This is the opposite of barrier free. This kind of language is not

05:00.456 --> 05:04.020
just stigmatizing, it actively deters people from applying.

05:04.770 --> 05:09.386
Even worse, many of these questions are repeated in slightly different forms throughout the application,

05:09.578 --> 05:13.790
especially when addressing sensory, cognitive or mental health related disabilities.

05:14.130 --> 05:17.434
This repetition doesn't improve clarity. In fact, it works to

05:17.442 --> 05:21.466
reinforce harmful stereotypes about people with disabilities being dangerous,

05:21.578 --> 05:26.030
confused or incapable. The effect is dehumanizing and exhausting.

05:26.770 --> 05:30.026
I personally have an application for my son Holden, left half filled

05:30.058 --> 05:34.168
out because of how depressing the application process is is he's

05:34.184 --> 05:37.768
a kid who's accomplished so much in life. But in order to use public transit,

05:37.864 --> 05:42.300
I as his father have to sit through a lengthy application that undermines all visibilities

05:42.720 --> 05:46.648
and the reason why I have to reapply is because GRT will cancel

05:46.664 --> 05:50.380
your pass if you do not use it enough without any notice to the passholder.

05:52.080 --> 05:55.336
If the GRT business plan wants to deliver a rider experience

05:55.408 --> 05:58.264
that is intuitive, customer focused and simple,

05:58.392 --> 06:01.820
we need a registration process that doesn't feel like an interrogation.

06:02.790 --> 06:06.286
Another significant barrier lies in who is allowed to complete

06:06.318 --> 06:10.190
the application. The required health disability form

06:10.310 --> 06:13.566
can only be filled out by a regulated health professional or an ACE

06:13.598 --> 06:17.342
teacher. This excludes applicants who don't have regular access to

06:17.366 --> 06:21.454
a doctor or nurse practitioner rely on peer support caseworkers,

06:21.582 --> 06:25.374
indigenous elders or community based care Live with disabilities

06:25.422 --> 06:28.410
that aren't easily assessed through clinical clinical tools.

06:28.910 --> 06:32.374
This approach reinforces a medical gatekeeping model rather

06:32.422 --> 06:34.650
than embracing a social model of disability.

06:36.110 --> 06:40.566
We also want to address a concern raised by GRT that some

06:40.718 --> 06:44.198
applicants may be applying to Mobility plus solely to access free

06:44.254 --> 06:47.862
conventional transit service. The solution proposed is

06:47.886 --> 06:51.206
to invest in a way to track people with disabilities use of conventional public

06:51.278 --> 06:55.050
transit to make sure they aren't taking advantage of free rides.

06:55.390 --> 06:59.410
While we understand the need to manage resources responsibly this

06:59.710 --> 07:03.638
concern should not be used to tighten access or introduce greater scrutiny

07:03.654 --> 07:06.290
in an already burdensome process.

07:08.030 --> 07:11.350
People with disabilities often face financial insecurity and

07:11.390 --> 07:15.350
accessing affordable accessible transit should be seen as a success of

07:15.390 --> 07:18.982
inclusive policy, not a loophole to be closed. Equity must

07:19.006 --> 07:22.102
come before efficiency. The assumption that people are

07:22.126 --> 07:25.894
applying to Mobility plus just to get free rides positions residents

07:25.942 --> 07:29.766
with disabilities as opportunistic. Rather than recognizing

07:29.798 --> 07:33.030
the real and valid reasons why people with disabilities rely on fare free

07:33.070 --> 07:35.862
transit, many face chronic poverty,

07:35.926 --> 07:39.622
unemployment and systemic exclusion, especially those with

07:39.646 --> 07:43.862
episodic, invisible or mental health disabilities who may not look

07:43.886 --> 07:47.370
disabled or fit institutional expectations.

07:48.190 --> 07:51.462
People with disabilities often move fluidly between specialized

07:51.526 --> 07:54.710
and conventional transit depending on their condition, the weather,

07:54.790 --> 07:57.690
energy levels or the accessibility of routes.

07:58.510 --> 08:02.006
Applying for Mobility plus does not mean someone is faking a need just to ride

08:02.038 --> 08:05.526
the bus for free. It may mean that they need both options to participate

08:05.558 --> 08:08.490
in communities safely and flexibly.

08:09.390 --> 08:13.190
We recommend the following changes. You replace deficit based

08:13.230 --> 08:17.068
language with strength based respectful alternatives to eliminate

08:17.164 --> 08:20.120
ableist framing and ensure it reflects dignity and respect.

08:21.140 --> 08:24.652
Eliminate or consolidate redundant stigmatizing.

08:24.716 --> 08:28.284
Questions? 30 seconds. Expand. Who can verify?

08:28.412 --> 08:31.724
Devin, just to interrupt. Sorry, you got about 30 seconds. Okay,

08:31.772 --> 08:35.132
okay. And affirm both

08:35.156 --> 08:38.240
in policy and public messaging that specialized transit is a valid

08:38.980 --> 08:42.502
mode of transportation, not a last resort. And if

08:42.526 --> 08:46.406
we want to truly empower more people to move freely in our communities, especially residents

08:46.438 --> 08:50.246
with disabilities, seniors and caregivers, we must affirm Mobility plus

08:50.278 --> 08:53.478
as a first class service. Thank you for your time. Thank you,

08:53.534 --> 08:57.446
Devin. We do have a few questions from Councilors Councillor Huneck.

08:57.558 --> 09:01.462
Thank you, Councillor James. Thank you, Devin and Holden for coming today and for

09:01.566 --> 09:04.010
outlining your concerns so eloquently.

09:05.550 --> 09:09.062
Chair James, I think it would be really helpful to Devin and

09:09.086 --> 09:12.236
Holden if Commissioner Spooner could address some of

09:12.308 --> 09:15.356
his concerns. Yep. Okay, go ahead.

09:15.428 --> 09:18.732
Yeah, through you, Chair. Thank you, Councillor. And thank you Devon and your family for

09:18.756 --> 09:22.236
coming in today. I think I'll start by saying I'm

09:22.268 --> 09:25.612
first. I'm extremely proud of the work we've done on Mobility plus in the

09:25.636 --> 09:29.628
last two years. This council has asked for a target on undelivered

09:29.644 --> 09:32.764
trips at 2.5% and we've achieved that.

09:32.932 --> 09:36.172
We've refreshed the software, trying to make better use of our resources. That is

09:36.196 --> 09:40.316
complete and we're about to renew our software. The scoping for that is underway

09:40.508 --> 09:43.372
and I think you said in the MP plan, you can see what we're trying

09:43.396 --> 09:46.476
to get at, right? It's more service, it's more customer

09:46.508 --> 09:50.252
focused service and really it's better service for

09:50.276 --> 09:53.564
our customers with an accessibility requirement. In terms of

09:53.572 --> 09:56.652
the application, we did some work on it through the last year,

09:56.756 --> 10:00.028
ready to do more. I'll reach out and have a chat with you. I'm interested

10:00.044 --> 10:03.292
in that. We are using an industry standard form and you

10:03.316 --> 10:06.732
got it right with the resources component. That is why we use

10:06.836 --> 10:10.396
that form and the eligibility. And it's a. The subsidy

10:10.428 --> 10:14.728
on a Mobility plus trip is five times that of a conventional trip. So we're

10:14.744 --> 10:17.992
bringing that number down. And so it's the right time to take a look

10:18.016 --> 10:21.512
at that application and see if it's achieving what it needs to be, which is

10:21.536 --> 10:24.552
again making sure that it's the people who need those trips who are getting that

10:24.576 --> 10:28.632
door to door service. In terms of the policy review about free

10:28.736 --> 10:32.504
rides on conventional transit. So policy

10:32.592 --> 10:35.816
decisions sit at this council, they don't sit with my team. So what we're suggesting

10:35.848 --> 10:38.874
is just a policy review. Two quick reasons for that. One,

10:39.032 --> 10:42.950
the first is right now we are one of only two agencies in Ontario

10:43.110 --> 10:46.790
that provide that. Soon we'll be the only one. And the second

10:46.830 --> 10:50.790
one is we've actually had customers tell us that during the application or

10:50.910 --> 10:54.630
eligibility process. So they've said to us, I'm not interested in Mobility Plus, I would

10:54.670 --> 10:57.830
simply like to have the free conventional. And so, you know, those two things put

10:57.870 --> 11:01.302
together. Appreciating that, that's a bit of a different perspective than you got.

11:01.326 --> 11:04.390
But I think those two reasons together tell me that we should do a quick

11:04.430 --> 11:08.092
review and bring it back to council for consideration. So all

11:08.116 --> 11:11.164
that to be said, I think you've hit some really good points.

11:11.252 --> 11:13.692
I'm looking at my team in the crowd and nodding along. We've got some work

11:13.716 --> 11:16.604
to do, so I'm happy to reach out and set something up to have a

11:16.612 --> 11:20.380
look both at the application and how we can make the overall system work better.

11:20.420 --> 11:23.100
So thank you. Thank you,

11:23.220 --> 11:24.640
Councillor Wolf.

11:26.500 --> 11:29.996
Through you, Madam Chair. Well, Devin and Holden,

11:30.028 --> 11:33.772
I just like to thank you for coming from Cambridge

11:33.916 --> 11:37.660
and I know how much work

11:37.700 --> 11:41.892
you've done in Cambridge to promote accessibility and

11:42.076 --> 11:45.412
we're thankful. And I was wondering if you could leave your presentation

11:45.476 --> 11:48.836
with us because you brought out many points that I wasn't

11:48.868 --> 11:52.916
aware of or problems that I didn't

11:52.948 --> 11:56.660
know about. Like for instance, that the pass expires and

11:56.700 --> 11:59.972
people wouldn't know that their pass had expired, that kind

11:59.996 --> 12:04.148
of thing. So. And I definitely think,

12:04.204 --> 12:08.400
as Commissioner Spooner has said, that the

12:08.860 --> 12:12.340
wording on the application could be more positive

12:12.420 --> 12:16.260
and emphasize people's Strengths, not their weaknesses. So I think

12:16.300 --> 12:19.920
that's something we can definitely, definitely use.

12:20.220 --> 12:23.588
I could definitely email it over. Okay, thanks.

12:23.764 --> 12:27.268
Thank you. And clerks. He will follow up for his

12:27.404 --> 12:31.012
written notes. Okay, thank you, Devin. Next we

12:31.036 --> 12:35.290
have Lori Strauss in person.

12:36.630 --> 12:38.690
Perfect. Laurie, you have five minutes.

12:45.110 --> 12:48.574
Good evening, Madam Chairman and councillors and staff.

12:48.742 --> 12:52.350
Thank you so much for offering the public input on the

12:52.390 --> 12:55.870
GRT program. My maiden name is Hefner.

12:55.950 --> 12:59.854
I grew up in the family dealership. I have become an automotive writer.

12:59.982 --> 13:04.206
But as life would have it, I was born with a rare genetic

13:04.238 --> 13:07.886
form of epilepsy. Thankfully, it's very mild. It means I can't drive.

13:08.078 --> 13:11.550
So life has been interesting. But it also means I've been using

13:11.590 --> 13:15.502
transit for quite some time. Within the big

13:15.526 --> 13:18.750
plans. Sometimes it can be difficult to remember

13:18.830 --> 13:22.590
the smaller details. And when I've seen the discussions about

13:22.630 --> 13:26.600
transit in the paper, listening to friends,

13:26.720 --> 13:30.344
anybody who drives, I find it's often,

13:30.512 --> 13:34.200
you know, car users, car drivers versus public

13:34.240 --> 13:37.800
transit users. And I'm not sure how much overlap there is between those who actually

13:37.840 --> 13:41.432
own a car and use transit. So there are just a few

13:41.456 --> 13:43.940
things I just wanted to mention that may be of use.

13:44.960 --> 13:48.360
Starting the buses. There have been huge improvements to the buses over

13:48.400 --> 13:51.272
the years. As much as I love being an 8 year old walking up the

13:51.296 --> 13:55.140
steps in the orange buses, accessibility these days is much, much better.

13:56.670 --> 14:00.198
I would ask you to consider the chair,

14:00.334 --> 14:04.166
the seats opposite the back doors. In future

14:04.238 --> 14:07.942
buses, it might be useful to have those removed. And the reason why I

14:07.966 --> 14:11.414
suggest that is the buses are not friendly when you have

14:11.502 --> 14:15.062
luggage with you, when you have your shopping cart with you. And for

14:15.086 --> 14:18.550
those of us who need the bus to do our errands, we're suddenly taking up

14:18.590 --> 14:22.072
aisle space with our larger pieces and having somewhere to

14:22.096 --> 14:25.320
put them that we can actually be next to them and out of the way

14:25.440 --> 14:26.620
would be helpful.

14:28.640 --> 14:31.048
Drivers and those of us who drive with drivers,

14:31.144 --> 14:34.888
passengers. A car offers a certain amount of privacy and

14:34.944 --> 14:38.232
certainly when you're on transit, you try and keep your

14:38.256 --> 14:40.792
own privacy. For others, privacy is not that important.

14:40.976 --> 14:44.040
And so you're listening to someone else's videos, someone else's music,

14:44.160 --> 14:47.400
both sides of the phone conversation. And if there is a way to

14:47.440 --> 14:50.836
appropriately enforce more communal use of transit,

14:50.868 --> 14:53.880
I think that might encourage more people to participate.

14:54.860 --> 14:58.388
The stations themselves, the platforms. Again,

14:58.444 --> 15:01.684
I'm seeing huge improvements. More shelters, the LRT

15:01.732 --> 15:05.460
platforms having heating in there. Many positives there.

15:05.500 --> 15:09.460
However, I think we can agree that the technology isn't as reliable

15:09.540 --> 15:13.396
as one would like. Sometimes the vending machines don't

15:13.428 --> 15:16.644
work and then you're stuck wondering if you should get on the LRT knowing you

15:16.652 --> 15:20.164
tried to pay but couldn't. I have five cards myself

15:20.212 --> 15:23.264
because I've been stuck with the auto load not working and then kicked off the

15:23.272 --> 15:26.976
bus. Understandably, I couldn't pay the fare, but it was because the auto load didn't

15:27.008 --> 15:31.136
auto load onto my card. So for new transit users,

15:31.168 --> 15:34.820
they're not going to purchase five EasyGo cards, you know, as backups.

15:35.640 --> 15:39.616
The parking is certainly an issue, but from the standpoint of there isn't

15:39.648 --> 15:43.220
always enough. The VIA station I think is probably the biggest example.

15:43.640 --> 15:47.376
20 parking spots or so. I don't know there's much surveillance there.

15:47.448 --> 15:51.376
And given the extremely high increase in car thefts these days,

15:51.448 --> 15:55.440
I do believe many drivers are discouraged from using Go Transit

15:55.600 --> 15:59.456
because they can't even park to begin with, even accessing

15:59.488 --> 16:03.200
it as a pedestrian. The stretch from King street

16:03.240 --> 16:06.864
to Victoria, the sidewalk is really narrow. There's one spot where

16:06.872 --> 16:09.500
there's a post right there. The post can't be removed. I understand,

16:10.360 --> 16:13.728
but a gentleman and I were kind of laughing at that spot. In the dead

16:13.744 --> 16:17.422
of winter, we had a lot of snow. There was a, for me, knee high

16:17.526 --> 16:21.454
packed mound of snow on the sidewalk right there that I don't

16:21.462 --> 16:23.022
know if it's the city of Kitchener, I don't know if it's a region.

16:23.046 --> 16:26.862
Whoever's responsible for that stretch didn't remove, so we're climbing over it

16:27.046 --> 16:30.030
with our bags or what have you because your other option is to walk into

16:30.070 --> 16:33.662
oncoming traffic. So again, these details that as

16:33.686 --> 16:37.214
we grow bigger and we dream bigger and we make all these major improvements

16:37.262 --> 16:40.910
to transit, we don't want to forget the smaller details that may

16:41.030 --> 16:44.190
be barriers to those who have the access to cars to actually

16:44.230 --> 16:47.804
give transit a try. The last suggestion I

16:47.812 --> 16:52.188
would have is communication not from the grt.

16:52.284 --> 16:56.364
Overall, it's been fantastic. But within the buses directly,

16:56.492 --> 16:59.916
they do announce the transit stops. That's fantastic.

17:00.108 --> 17:03.772
But it would be very helpful if when the bus pulls up to a transfer

17:03.836 --> 17:07.532
point that it also explains what other routes are

17:07.556 --> 17:11.160
available there. So if you're newer to the system or the system

17:11.540 --> 17:13.840
has undergone a lot of flux lately,

17:14.760 --> 17:18.176
that it's automatically announced that you're coming up to King

17:18.208 --> 17:21.720
and University station. Transfers to routes 8,

17:21.853 --> 17:25.420
12, 29 and other routes I don't remember right now.

17:25.720 --> 17:29.072
So anyways, those are the points I wanted to make. Thank you very much for

17:29.096 --> 17:32.448
your time and I look forward to hearing other feedback from everyone

17:32.504 --> 17:35.840
else participating in tonight's discussion. Excellent. Thank you, Laurie. We have

17:35.880 --> 17:38.900
no questions. Thank you for taking the time to come in tonight.

17:39.900 --> 17:43.972
Next we have Michael Druker. You're here in person.

17:44.076 --> 17:45.880
You have five minutes, Michael.

18:01.820 --> 18:05.332
Madam Chair, members of committee, thank you for the opportunity to speak here

18:05.356 --> 18:07.500
tonight. Is your mic on? Yeah.

18:09.680 --> 18:12.420
How about now? All right, thank you.

18:13.200 --> 18:16.664
All right. Madam Chair, members of committee, thank you for the

18:16.672 --> 18:18.980
opportunity to speak here tonight. Next slide, please.

18:21.040 --> 18:24.232
I'm here on behalf of the Tri Cities Transport Action Group, a community group

18:24.256 --> 18:27.432
which would like to see it made easier, more pleasant for more

18:27.456 --> 18:31.140
people to choose walking, biking and transit to get around our region.

18:32.800 --> 18:36.264
And I'm here to indicate support for the plan and also a

18:36.272 --> 18:39.140
few ideas for improvements. Next slide, please.

18:40.000 --> 18:43.704
As you know, not just ion, but GRT itself is part

18:43.712 --> 18:47.336
of an effort to better align land use and planning. The creation of Grand River

18:47.368 --> 18:50.616
Transit in the year 2000 enabled rapid transit

18:50.648 --> 18:54.020
planning along the central transit corridor just a few years later.

18:55.040 --> 18:58.750
And that's been part of shaping land use quite a bit.

18:58.840 --> 19:02.978
When the region did build rapid transit, it didn't just build the first phase

19:02.994 --> 19:06.834
of ion, it also redesigned the bus routes and

19:06.922 --> 19:09.710
invested in service increases to make a better network.

19:10.410 --> 19:14.350
So we have seen ION attract billions of dollars in development investment

19:14.650 --> 19:18.466
in the central core, and much of the growth is now in the central transit

19:18.498 --> 19:22.114
corridor, the station areas, and that's great. Transit as

19:22.122 --> 19:25.240
a percentage of trips has grown. It's great.

19:26.020 --> 19:29.180
You can't say that for many peer cities during

19:29.220 --> 19:32.280
the pandemic that's been happening here. It's great.

19:33.140 --> 19:36.540
However, the transit percentage is still low and most trips in the region

19:36.580 --> 19:40.060
are still made by car. We do need to continue to make it better,

19:40.100 --> 19:43.756
to make it competitive, and we need to do it with some urgency

19:43.788 --> 19:47.292
because the alternative is not just worse for the environment and worse for

19:47.316 --> 19:51.604
our health. The alternative is also more expensive road expansion.

19:51.732 --> 19:54.804
As I think we all remember, preventing road expansion was a

19:54.812 --> 19:57.924
big aspect of the plans for ion, and that's still true.

19:57.972 --> 20:01.572
It's really expensive and good transit is part of the story to

20:01.596 --> 20:05.780
avoid it. Next slide, please. So the

20:05.820 --> 20:09.412
frequent service network in the GRT business plan is a site

20:09.436 --> 20:12.500
for sore eyes. Today there's a handful of routes with

20:12.540 --> 20:16.206
15 minute service, but only on weekdays. Most routes

20:16.238 --> 20:19.518
are 30 minutes outside of peak hours. Only Route

20:19.573 --> 20:23.050
7 and ION are actually frequent all week.

20:24.070 --> 20:27.454
You've heard frequency is freedom. It's really hard to plan

20:27.542 --> 20:31.730
your life around using transit if you have to plan your day around using transit.

20:32.630 --> 20:36.366
So creating an expansive frequent service network

20:36.518 --> 20:40.014
would be a game changer. You don't need to worry about schedules.

20:40.142 --> 20:43.678
It's much more easy to transfer from one route to another to get way across

20:43.734 --> 20:47.404
town. And again, this is transformative.

20:47.452 --> 20:51.212
And I would even say that ridership increases from this are probably understated in the

20:51.236 --> 20:54.796
plans. So there's many other changes along the lines

20:54.828 --> 20:58.236
of just the basic utility of transit in the plans

20:58.268 --> 21:01.484
that are really great. But I want

21:01.492 --> 21:04.080
to speak on a couple of other issues. Next slide.

21:06.660 --> 21:10.252
Now a caution. The business plan is

21:10.276 --> 21:13.292
not fully clear on this, but I'm sure there will be pressure every year to

21:13.316 --> 21:17.052
to squeeze more revenue out of buses, including through wrapping them in ads. This is

21:17.076 --> 21:20.240
a mistake if you're trying to get people to take transit.

21:20.980 --> 21:24.620
Your ability to reach climate goals, avoid road

21:24.660 --> 21:28.588
expansion, guide new development depends on more people wanting to take transit.

21:28.764 --> 21:31.644
Don't make transit vehicles into moving billboards.

21:31.772 --> 21:35.600
Especially don't wrap the windows. It's unpleasant, it's nauseating.

21:35.940 --> 21:39.004
And what message does it send when you cover the windows on

21:39.012 --> 21:42.600
a bus full of people to get a few bucks from a car dealership's ad?

21:44.050 --> 21:47.770
So, a request on fair

21:47.810 --> 21:50.750
strategies and sorry on revenue strategies.

21:51.410 --> 21:52.630
Next slide, please.

21:53.810 --> 21:57.386
Finally, we need to seriously rethink how we're

21:57.418 --> 22:00.810
approaching transit expansion. Ion stage two is taking a long

22:00.850 --> 22:04.394
time and with current costs and approaches, it might take forever. We need to

22:04.402 --> 22:07.482
be looking at phases three, four and five. We need to be able to build

22:07.506 --> 22:11.550
incrementally and if we had been doing that already,

22:11.670 --> 22:15.262
we probably would be under construction for phase two. Costs have gone up

22:15.286 --> 22:18.190
in many places in Canada and the U.S. and it's not mostly inflation.

22:18.270 --> 22:21.950
It's poor practices in which agencies and governments make construction someone

22:21.990 --> 22:26.030
else's problem and have little oversight for cost control and competitiveness

22:26.190 --> 22:29.550
for the details of the plan. Transit isn't that

22:29.590 --> 22:33.326
different from the other areas where we do have in house capacity to oversee

22:33.358 --> 22:36.440
projects. Roadworks, utility relocation.

22:36.520 --> 22:40.696
We do lots of public works. But for transit,

22:40.888 --> 22:44.120
we and many others have decided we

22:44.160 --> 22:47.736
are not competent to do that. And so that has proven

22:47.768 --> 22:50.792
to be a mistake, a very costly mistake. We need to remedy that.

22:50.896 --> 22:55.320
Let's learn from what works better elsewhere and work better here and

22:55.360 --> 22:59.320
fund in house capacity for transit infrastructure, planning, procurement and

22:59.360 --> 23:03.834
management. We would be able to get the cost down, build proactively and

23:04.002 --> 23:06.870
get much more built for the same amount of money.

23:08.370 --> 23:12.826
You have about 15 seconds or so, actually. Yeah, just summarize,

23:12.938 --> 23:15.750
summarizing. Next slide, please.

23:16.050 --> 23:19.910
This plan includes many excellent elements and

23:20.210 --> 23:23.750
we would like to see it implemented as soon as possible. Thank you.

23:27.420 --> 23:30.840
Thank you. I do Have a question from Councilor Banovic.

23:31.820 --> 23:35.092
Thanks. Not so much a question as really just a note of thanks

23:35.156 --> 23:38.772
to you and everyone at TRITAG for all you

23:38.796 --> 23:41.876
do, whether it's on transit, on cycling, on trails,

23:41.908 --> 23:45.876
and all the things that we're doing to provide

23:46.028 --> 23:50.244
active transportation, other transportation choices for people. Thank you,

23:50.412 --> 23:54.032
Councillor McCabe. Thank you. Through you, Chair, thanks for coming

23:54.056 --> 23:57.232
in. Michael, nice to see you again. I'm just curious about what you were saying

23:57.256 --> 24:00.960
about the ads. And I mean, I understand from the

24:01.000 --> 24:04.528
accessibility piece and it covering windows,

24:04.704 --> 24:08.380
but if it's just on the panels or the back,

24:10.920 --> 24:14.160
is that an issue for you? And I

24:14.200 --> 24:18.016
was also curious, when you use the word nauseating, do you mean you just don't

24:18.048 --> 24:21.630
like it or it actually it's physically with the,

24:21.670 --> 24:24.690
I don't know, the movement. Okay,

24:25.430 --> 24:31.022
yes. So let me go in order. So I

24:31.046 --> 24:35.410
think ads that cover windows are a different category

24:35.750 --> 24:37.770
than ads in general.

24:39.350 --> 24:42.910
I think the considerations, I would say for ads in general, especially outside

24:42.950 --> 24:46.830
of the vehicles, are around the prestige of transit and how

24:46.870 --> 24:51.110
you. How you brand transit as

24:51.150 --> 24:54.902
a mode of travel. And so that's very reasonable. But I think

24:55.006 --> 24:58.934
covering the windows is worse because I definitely

24:58.982 --> 25:03.222
do know that people find it physically

25:03.286 --> 25:06.130
nauseating. Sometimes when it's hard to see outside,

25:06.510 --> 25:09.862
especially when it's dark, people find it difficult to see where they

25:09.886 --> 25:13.160
are. So it's a problem. And it's

25:13.230 --> 25:17.236
better than when the wrap completely covers

25:17.268 --> 25:20.260
the window. But even as is, it covers much of the light.

25:20.380 --> 25:23.320
So it's a qualitative difference.

25:24.860 --> 25:28.484
I may have forgotten the second question. Okay, thank you.

25:28.652 --> 25:31.640
Okay, thank you, Michael.

25:31.980 --> 25:38.292
We will now call on. Bruno should

25:38.316 --> 25:41.160
be on. Good evening. So you have five minutes, Bruno.

25:41.640 --> 25:45.424
Thank you. Good evening, Chair, council and staff.

25:45.592 --> 25:49.744
Today I will be speaking in favor of the GRT's business plan.

25:49.912 --> 25:53.680
So one of the reasons that I came to Waterloo region to

25:53.720 --> 25:57.152
live was because I saw that it was building forward

25:57.336 --> 26:01.100
with its public and active transportation system.

26:01.400 --> 26:04.608
And this is really important for me because this

26:04.664 --> 26:07.792
transit system is a lifeline to do anything

26:07.856 --> 26:11.728
in this region. So I cannot walk, bike or

26:11.784 --> 26:14.912
take transit. I have no other option to

26:14.936 --> 26:18.400
get around unless I want to pay thousands or

26:18.440 --> 26:21.776
more for a car or rely

26:21.808 --> 26:25.264
on others to drive me. And I

26:25.272 --> 26:28.992
just can't help but wonder how many people have to make a

26:29.016 --> 26:32.288
choice between paying their car bills or other

26:32.344 --> 26:35.818
necessities. I. I can't help but wonder

26:35.914 --> 26:39.910
how many people could be using the money they spend on the car,

26:40.370 --> 26:44.682
their goods and services that they need or want.

26:44.866 --> 26:49.050
And now that Waterloo region was identified by

26:49.090 --> 26:53.402
the Canadian Chamber of Commerce to be the most vulnerable

26:53.466 --> 26:57.066
community to tariffs. Improving affordability

26:57.178 --> 27:00.602
is even more critical. And I would say

27:00.626 --> 27:03.830
that transit is the most affordable way to get around.

27:04.370 --> 27:08.522
And the improvements in the GRT's business plan could

27:08.546 --> 27:12.090
help to empower people like me who enjoy being able to

27:12.130 --> 27:16.074
take public and active transportation and

27:16.162 --> 27:20.074
others who would if the service coverage was

27:20.242 --> 27:23.770
better, more frequent and more reliable,

27:23.930 --> 27:27.642
especially in the more suburban and rural parts of the

27:27.666 --> 27:31.322
region. So I think this plan is a step in

27:31.346 --> 27:34.954
the right direction and can help the Waterloo region

27:35.042 --> 27:39.786
to prosper by improving affordability and mobility.

27:39.978 --> 27:43.386
Thank you. Thank you, Bruno.

27:43.418 --> 27:47.162
I see no questions from councilors. Thank you for taking the time to

27:47.346 --> 27:51.002
show your support tonight. Next, I have Brian Paul.

27:51.066 --> 27:53.790
I think you're in person. You have five minutes.

27:58.060 --> 28:02.356
Good evening. I am Brian Paul, the executive director for supportive housing in Waterloo,

28:02.388 --> 28:05.652
also known as show. Thank you for the opportunity to speak with you

28:05.676 --> 28:09.716
tonight. We are at an important moment as GRT develops

28:09.748 --> 28:14.068
this 10 year plan. I was happy to see that children were included, but disheartened

28:14.084 --> 28:17.332
that older adults were left out. This is a missed opportunity that we

28:17.356 --> 28:20.724
can still fix. As our community ages.

28:20.772 --> 28:24.432
Affordable transit for older adults is more urgent than ever. They are

28:24.456 --> 28:27.792
now the fastest growing demographic in the shelter system.

28:27.976 --> 28:31.616
We are seeing the impact firsthand. Since 2023,

28:31.768 --> 28:35.440
the number of older adults experiencing homelessness in the region has increased by

28:35.480 --> 28:39.232
55%. And our waitlist for those who are 65+ has

28:39.256 --> 28:41.980
more than doubled in just this last year.

28:42.440 --> 28:46.624
That's why I'm here to propose that free Transit for older adults, 65 plus be

28:46.632 --> 28:50.240
included in GRT's 10 year plan. This is not just about

28:50.280 --> 28:53.876
transit. It is a lifeline. Free transit would remove a major

28:53.948 --> 28:57.412
financial barrier for older adults. As shown on the

28:57.436 --> 29:00.884
screen, free transit is already standard practice elsewhere.

29:00.932 --> 29:03.732
This is not a new idea. It's a proven one.

29:03.916 --> 29:07.028
Research shows that mobility is closely linked to well

29:07.084 --> 29:10.356
being. For older adults, transit means more than movement.

29:10.468 --> 29:13.076
It's a key to independence, opportunity,

29:13.188 --> 29:16.660
connection and aging with dignity. The current

29:16.700 --> 29:20.054
lack of affordable transit fuels social isolation, a growing

29:20.102 --> 29:23.830
public health concern tied to depression, cognitive decline and

29:23.870 --> 29:27.286
hospitalizations. Now, I understand that free transit

29:27.318 --> 29:30.534
is not truly free. It's funded through taxes,

29:30.582 --> 29:34.470
government support, advertising and fares. But in other municipalities,

29:34.550 --> 29:37.478
this investment has paid off. With higher ridership,

29:37.574 --> 29:40.422
stronger economies and new funding.

29:40.566 --> 29:44.566
The results speak for themselves. Mississauga saw a 30% increase

29:44.638 --> 29:47.320
in ridership among older adults. In Kingston,

29:47.400 --> 29:50.888
40%. And in Oakville, they saw a 578%

29:50.944 --> 29:54.824
increase. Other municipalities have seen a 20 to 30% increase

29:54.912 --> 29:57.740
in overall ridership. With this initiative,

30:00.480 --> 30:04.168
older adults who can move more freely, shop more, attend more events

30:04.184 --> 30:07.976
and support local businesses, strengthening the economy around transit

30:08.008 --> 30:11.960
corridors. GRT's Affordable Transit Program offers

30:12.040 --> 30:15.300
income based discounts, but many older adults living on CPP

30:15.380 --> 30:19.124
and OAS have an income of just about $26,000

30:19.172 --> 30:22.996
a year. While qualifying under this program, the almost $50

30:23.028 --> 30:26.452
a month in fare is still a major, major burden for them.

30:26.556 --> 30:30.212
Transit must compete with essentials like food, housing and rising

30:30.276 --> 30:33.588
medical costs. In the region, 35% of older

30:33.604 --> 30:37.172
adults rely solely on CPP OAS and have

30:37.196 --> 30:41.102
no additional family support. The current ATP program

30:41.206 --> 30:44.238
is insufficient and leaves many older adults behind.

30:44.374 --> 30:47.854
Free transit is the solution that aligns with all of our shared

30:47.902 --> 30:51.230
goals of creating and making transit more accessible and

30:51.270 --> 30:54.414
more equitable. And while the seniors transit

30:54.462 --> 30:57.582
tax credit is helpful, a one time tax refund at

30:57.606 --> 31:01.374
tax season is not a substitute for daily, reliable and affordable

31:01.422 --> 31:05.006
transit. It's also been noted that GRT ridership

31:05.038 --> 31:08.432
is flattening flattening throughout the

31:08.456 --> 31:12.256
day. Which is exactly why free transit for older adults makes sense.

31:12.368 --> 31:15.980
It would make better use of non peak hours with little added cost.

31:16.600 --> 31:20.256
And although the 20192020 data shows

31:20.288 --> 31:24.528
higher average incomes for older adults, averages are misleading.

31:24.704 --> 31:28.832
It's a myth that people who are retired are financially secure.

31:28.976 --> 31:32.820
Four out of the last five older adults that we've housed at show had never

31:32.860 --> 31:35.680
experienced homelessness before in their life.

31:36.220 --> 31:40.100
Burlington, Oakville, Brampton and many others already offer free

31:40.140 --> 31:43.668
transit based on public benefit. We must ask

31:43.724 --> 31:47.572
is transit a service for community well being or a

31:47.596 --> 31:51.348
business focused on cost recovery? Many other communities have already

31:51.404 --> 31:55.044
chosen service and it is paying off for them. It's time for GRT to do

31:55.052 --> 31:59.082
the same. Free transit for older adults is good for the grt,

31:59.196 --> 32:03.054
it's good for businesses and it's good for the region. Our aging

32:03.102 --> 32:06.462
population have earned this and they deserve this. Thank you

32:06.486 --> 32:09.774
for your time. Thank you Brian. Don't go anywhere.

32:09.822 --> 32:13.694
We have a couple of questions from councillors. Councillor McCabe, thank you

32:13.782 --> 32:17.502
through Chair Thanks Brian for coming in and for the work

32:17.526 --> 32:21.022
that you do with some of our most vulnerable seniors and

32:21.126 --> 32:24.654
others in our community with the supportive Housing of Waterloo

32:24.702 --> 32:28.630
program and your team does great work. I'm just

32:29.090 --> 32:33.802
curious because are

32:33.826 --> 32:37.114
you aware of the affordable transit pass or program

32:37.282 --> 32:41.130
that the region has? Because when we met, some of us,

32:41.250 --> 32:44.150
Councillor Erb and Councillor Huneck and I met,

32:44.690 --> 32:48.554
it was about February last year with a variety of nonprofits and

32:48.642 --> 32:52.042
they weren't aware that we have a pretty like

32:52.066 --> 32:55.510
a 50% or just a little bit more than 50% now.

32:55.920 --> 33:00.392
So I just wanted to make sure you were aware of that and

33:00.416 --> 33:03.816
I spoke to that. It's not enough for the individuals

33:03.928 --> 33:07.496
we've seen and for the majority of older adults in

33:07.568 --> 33:10.808
the region, I referenced the average.

33:10.984 --> 33:14.520
The reason why the average is higher than other groups is because there

33:14.560 --> 33:18.056
are a small subset of significantly high earners

33:18.168 --> 33:21.282
who skew those numbers. We are

33:21.306 --> 33:23.950
seeing more older adults struggling than ever before.

33:24.650 --> 33:28.418
Yeah, and I'm really glad you came in and presented about this because we

33:28.474 --> 33:32.290
had had a bit of a discussion I think of maybe a year

33:32.330 --> 33:35.970
or so ago around free

33:36.010 --> 33:39.122
transit for seniors, free transit for teenagers.

33:39.186 --> 33:43.750
Right. And if you had to choose, which one would you choose?

33:44.250 --> 33:47.682
And quite frankly I was thinking more youth, right. So that

33:47.706 --> 33:50.962
they could, you know, they can become future riders. And for

33:50.986 --> 33:54.770
lots and lots of reasons thinking that, you know, seniors have

33:54.810 --> 33:58.402
had the opportunity to do earn income, et cetera. So I

33:58.426 --> 34:02.354
appreciate you raising these issues and certainly gives us a lot more, a lot to

34:02.442 --> 34:06.242
think about. So thank you very much. Thank you. Thank you. Councilor Huneck.

34:06.306 --> 34:09.250
Hi Brian, thank you for coming. It's great to see you. It's great to hear

34:09.290 --> 34:12.390
you. Thank you for advocating for seniors.

34:13.610 --> 34:17.802
I think one of the strengths of the GRT business plan

34:17.826 --> 34:21.482
that I see is that no matter what age or ability

34:21.626 --> 34:25.018
you have, if you need cheaper

34:25.194 --> 34:28.586
transit throughout our region, you will get it.

34:28.738 --> 34:32.266
And I know that you rightfully pointed

34:32.298 --> 34:36.186
out that even ATP is too high

34:36.338 --> 34:39.430
for a lot of the people that need it,

34:40.060 --> 34:44.532
which is why the business plan also speaks to tiered

34:44.676 --> 34:47.988
ATP. I don't know, Commissioner Spooner, if you

34:48.044 --> 34:51.572
want to elaborate a little bit on what

34:51.596 --> 34:55.540
that means, but that might help your cause,

34:55.620 --> 34:58.740
Brian, through your chair.

34:58.780 --> 35:01.840
Yeah, thank you, Councillor Hunek and Brian, thank you for coming in.

35:02.460 --> 35:05.652
So, yeah, you've hit the exact. What we feel like

35:05.676 --> 35:08.612
is the appropriate response through the business plan, Councilor Huneck. So,

35:08.716 --> 35:12.608
Brian, we recognize this issue as well, that the ATP program today,

35:12.744 --> 35:16.320
while it provides today a 50% discount as of July 1, will provide

35:16.360 --> 35:19.888
a 55% discount. It doesn't hit everybody.

35:20.064 --> 35:23.600
I like that program. I'll note that the agencies that you showed do group based

35:23.640 --> 35:26.784
fares. They do not do the Affordable Transit program. That is a decision that we

35:26.792 --> 35:30.448
have made. But the idea is that it's

35:30.464 --> 35:33.760
not binary. It's not do you need it or not? And so the idea of

35:33.800 --> 35:37.568
tiered ATP is, as you've pointed out, those who

35:37.624 --> 35:40.848
qualify for the market basket goods but actually need more support.

35:40.984 --> 35:44.492
We'd have the ability to ramp up that 55% discount amount to

35:44.516 --> 35:47.692
a 75 or we've got to do the work on, on what that looks like.

35:47.796 --> 35:50.680
But that's what we're looking at for the approach. Thank you.

35:52.020 --> 35:54.400
Thank you, Councilor Fox. I hope that helps.

35:56.820 --> 36:00.492
Thanks David, for coming in. And again, this has

36:00.516 --> 36:03.884
been talked about and talked about and talked about and, and you're absolutely right.

36:03.972 --> 36:07.340
And Mr. Spooner, I think has done the right thing in

36:07.380 --> 36:10.822
my opinion. And what we want to do is to serve the

36:10.846 --> 36:14.262
people that need it. Now the last time this came to regional council,

36:14.446 --> 36:17.990
I got quite a few emails from seniors saying I don't want the

36:18.030 --> 36:22.134
discount, but I'm willing to donate money to those that can't afford it.

36:22.302 --> 36:26.214
So they said that though there's a real mix here, a bag across

36:26.302 --> 36:29.862
the board to give a discount to somebody that has

36:29.886 --> 36:33.222
an income over 100,000 a year doesn't make a lot

36:33.246 --> 36:37.010
of sense to me. But to give it to people that really need it,

36:37.150 --> 36:40.410
you know, single parents, children with

36:40.450 --> 36:44.090
low income families, seniors that again are struggling.

36:44.250 --> 36:47.946
And I would even consider at some point if the household

36:47.978 --> 36:51.722
is poor enough that, that we do 100%. But I

36:51.746 --> 36:55.226
think we have to base it on real numbers realistically.

36:55.418 --> 36:58.090
But that's just my opinion. Thank you. Thank you,

36:58.210 --> 37:01.722
Councillor Wolf. Through you, Madam Chair.

37:01.906 --> 37:05.480
Brian, thank you for coming in. Another Cambridge person.

37:07.300 --> 37:09.520
I'm counting anyway.

37:10.980 --> 37:14.668
Have you as a first step or a step

37:14.804 --> 37:18.652
considered time of day free for

37:18.836 --> 37:22.960
seniors like 10 till 2 or Wednesdays?

37:23.380 --> 37:29.212
And I thought for me they

37:29.236 --> 37:33.372
could at least if, let's say it was Wednesday, people could try to get appointments

37:33.436 --> 37:36.864
on the Wednesday or time of day.

37:37.032 --> 37:40.032
What do you, what do you think about this? So that's why that slide I

37:40.056 --> 37:43.648
presented is so important, because it shows you different types of things you can

37:43.704 --> 37:47.184
do. This is you just have to look. So as I

37:47.352 --> 37:50.912
rely on, I want to thank my friends at the Waterloo Age

37:50.936 --> 37:54.224
Friendly Network who provided some of these stats for me. And as I

37:54.232 --> 37:57.248
was preparing this delegation I was like, whoa,

37:57.344 --> 38:00.556
okay. I mean, if others can do this, why can't we do this?

38:00.728 --> 38:03.972
There has to be some way of doing something like that. Maybe a free day

38:04.076 --> 38:07.796
a week or something. There has to be. If others can do it.

38:07.868 --> 38:10.596
We are a caring community, we're progressive.

38:10.708 --> 38:13.844
We should be thinking about, we should be doing things like this too. To me

38:13.852 --> 38:17.284
it's a no brainer. There's a need. We're constantly

38:17.332 --> 38:20.868
advocating about that need for seniors, for older adults

38:20.884 --> 38:24.100
who are struggling more than ever before. We have to do more to support them.

38:24.220 --> 38:28.034
There are other municipalities currently actively doing this.

38:28.122 --> 38:31.362
I listed them all to you. You have that slide. We should

38:31.386 --> 38:34.258
be doing something like that, too. We can do something like that, too.

38:34.394 --> 38:37.590
Okay, thank you. Thank you, Chair Redmond.

38:38.570 --> 38:41.810
Thank you so much for coming in. And I hope this doesn't end up being

38:41.850 --> 38:45.458
sort of a binary decision of yes or no. I think free transit's

38:45.474 --> 38:49.122
a wonderful concept, but I also, at budget time, know how much money we put

38:49.146 --> 38:53.030
into it. Do you fundamentally have a problem with the tiered

38:53.370 --> 38:56.670
support that Commissioner Spooner was talking about?

38:57.290 --> 39:00.450
This is the first time I've heard of it. I would be open to different

39:00.490 --> 39:04.546
ideas. I mean, I. I start. I've reached out to GRT to start a dialogue,

39:04.578 --> 39:08.110
to start a conversation, and that's what I'm looking to do. I mean,

39:09.370 --> 39:12.930
there are different things we can do. I'm not asking for free

39:12.970 --> 39:16.482
transit across the board for anyone over the age of 65. I want there

39:16.506 --> 39:20.190
to be something in the plan that speaks to this demographic and within that,

39:20.230 --> 39:23.774
we can work on something together. Listening to other

39:23.862 --> 39:27.662
people who live here, listening to organizations like show, and we

39:27.686 --> 39:31.422
can figure something out. I'm not saying across the board, 65 free

39:31.526 --> 39:35.470
plus, but we can look to see what others are doing, and we can do

39:35.510 --> 39:38.942
something like that here. I know we can. Thank you.

39:39.126 --> 39:43.182
Thank you, Brian. That's all for the questions tonight.

39:43.246 --> 39:46.510
Thank you for coming in. Next we have Ellie

39:46.590 --> 39:50.670
Liu, and then they are in

39:50.710 --> 39:52.650
person. Is Ellie here?

39:55.590 --> 39:58.770
No. Okay,

39:59.590 --> 40:03.810
we will move to the next delegation. Raj and David.

40:07.190 --> 40:08.850
And you have five minutes.

40:16.240 --> 40:19.512
Perfect. Can everyone hear me okay? Good evening,

40:19.576 --> 40:23.060
Chair James, Chair Redmond and members of Regional Council.

40:23.440 --> 40:26.632
My name is Raj. Sorry, Raj Desange.

40:26.696 --> 40:29.912
And I'm pleased to be here tonight on behalf of the Waterloo Undergraduate

40:29.976 --> 40:33.432
Student association at the University of Waterloo, otherwise known as

40:33.456 --> 40:36.536
wusa. I'm speaking on behalf of our Vice president,

40:36.608 --> 40:39.862
Arya Razimju. Unfortunately could not attend tonight.

40:40.006 --> 40:43.638
I'm currently in my third year of the legal Studies and Political Science program

40:43.694 --> 40:47.462
at uw and I'm glad to say I'm completing my co op term as a

40:47.486 --> 40:51.062
stakeholder Relations assistant with wusa. Transit is a

40:51.086 --> 40:54.774
major priority for students at the University of Waterloo. As a part of the UPASS

40:54.822 --> 40:58.102
working group, WUSA was glad to take part in consultations on this

40:58.126 --> 41:01.622
business plan. We appreciated the opportunity to share our perspective and

41:01.646 --> 41:04.608
work with regional staff. Over the past year,

41:04.664 --> 41:08.672
UW students have reimagined and sorry, have remained engaged in

41:08.696 --> 41:12.512
transit discussions. We supported the return of late night bus service and

41:12.536 --> 41:15.980
we were proud to lend our voice to that conversation right here at council.

41:16.680 --> 41:20.544
Earlier this year, during the regional budget process, our president, Nick Feifel,

41:20.592 --> 41:24.464
submitted a letter urging council not to move forward with proposed changes

41:24.512 --> 41:27.180
to routes, garbage collection and other services.

41:27.800 --> 41:31.002
Changes that would have since had a serious impact on students and

41:31.026 --> 41:34.666
overall ridership. We support the overall direction

41:34.698 --> 41:37.950
of the business plan, and we're hopeful about the improvements.

41:40.770 --> 41:43.818
Hello. The improvements it outlines.

41:43.914 --> 41:47.610
We look forward to working with regional staff on future initiatives that

41:47.650 --> 41:51.150
strengthens access to transit for our students and for all residents.

41:51.650 --> 41:55.386
We also understand, given today's economic challenges and tight municipal

41:55.418 --> 41:58.946
budgets, not everything in the plan will happen on schedule.

41:59.058 --> 42:02.834
But I would like to be clear. Students rely on transit,

42:02.962 --> 42:06.242
and through the UPASS program, transit relies on students as

42:06.266 --> 42:09.426
well. It's a partnership worth protecting and worth

42:09.458 --> 42:12.930
building on. As you consider this plan tonight and as you plan

42:12.970 --> 42:16.578
for the future, we ask that you continue to view students as both users and

42:16.634 --> 42:20.466
partners in the transit system. When considering students,

42:20.538 --> 42:23.692
it's important to remember that frequency matters, routing matters,

42:23.756 --> 42:25.964
especially when it comes to access to home,

42:26.132 --> 42:29.692
campus work and the places students need to be successful in their

42:29.716 --> 42:33.360
time here. And most importantly, safety always matters.

42:33.780 --> 42:37.420
The University of Waterloo has three campuses spread across the region.

42:37.540 --> 42:41.164
Our main campus in Waterloo, our School of Pharmacy in Kitchener, and the

42:41.172 --> 42:44.572
School of Architecture in Cambridge. Being so spread out means that

42:44.596 --> 42:49.280
students are regularly traveling between cities, and most of them rely entirely on transit.

42:49.710 --> 42:53.174
Because of how our campuses are distributed, transit ends up shaping where students

42:53.262 --> 42:56.930
live, where they can work, and how they take part in the broader community.

42:57.710 --> 43:01.238
When transit is infrequent or direct, it limits students options

43:01.334 --> 43:04.454
not just for their housing, but also for their co op placements,

43:04.582 --> 43:08.262
volunteer opportunities, and daily life. Students want

43:08.286 --> 43:11.734
to be active participants in this region, but to do that, they need reliable,

43:11.862 --> 43:14.490
accessible and affordable public transit.

43:15.000 --> 43:18.128
We appreciate that students were considered during the development of this plan,

43:18.184 --> 43:21.504
and we thank regional staff and council for recognizing the role students play in our

43:21.512 --> 43:24.896
transit system. That recognition matters and it's

43:24.928 --> 43:28.416
made a real difference. But as you consider this plan tonight, and as you shape

43:28.448 --> 43:32.128
future decisions, we urge you to continue keeping students top of mind.

43:32.264 --> 43:35.680
Thank you. Thank you. Raj and David.

43:35.840 --> 43:39.312
I see no questions from councillors. Thanks for coming in. Good to see

43:39.336 --> 43:44.638
you again. Thank you. Next will be Matthew Harvey in

43:44.694 --> 43:47.810
person. All right, Matthew, you have five minutes.

43:54.150 --> 43:57.358
Good evening, councillors and committee members. My name is Matthew Harvey

43:57.374 --> 44:00.638
and I've lived in the region for around 12 years. After reading

44:00.654 --> 44:04.142
the JRT business plan, I'm generally quite happy with it, and so

44:04.166 --> 44:07.140
I will try and Keep my comments brief and pointed here.

44:07.600 --> 44:11.016
As others have mentioned, there's a large omission from this plan

44:11.088 --> 44:15.660
that I want to make sure doesn't go underappreciated. Stage 2 ion

44:17.120 --> 44:21.260
and so I want to just take a moment to talk to you about dignity.

44:21.840 --> 44:26.056
There are myriad ways that other cities in North America generally use

44:26.208 --> 44:29.540
to make transit users feel as if they are doing the wrong thing,

44:30.080 --> 44:34.520
that they aren't valued and that they should be moving into

44:35.140 --> 44:38.360
the next phase where they get a car and do the right thing.

44:38.820 --> 44:42.124
Whether it's stops right next to high speed roads or vehicles

44:42.172 --> 44:45.644
with window coverings or poor

44:45.692 --> 44:48.480
ride quality and buses that get stuck in traffic,

44:49.140 --> 44:52.380
the message is clear. Our light

44:52.420 --> 44:56.124
rail is the antithesis to that. It smoothly cuts through traffic,

44:56.252 --> 45:00.172
is bright and wide and runs a predictable schedule.

45:00.236 --> 45:03.562
Generally our buses have good ways of

45:03.586 --> 45:07.626
enabling stroller and bicycle use, but the ION accommodates

45:07.658 --> 45:11.194
them effortlessly. The stations open directly onto pedestrian

45:11.242 --> 45:15.178
friendly areas and when they don't, they are at least as

45:15.234 --> 45:19.338
protected from traffic as they can be. It connects well to our bus network

45:19.474 --> 45:25.258
that is generally high quality and on time. And the

45:25.394 --> 45:28.714
frequent network in this plan, the proposed frequent network in

45:28.722 --> 45:32.582
this plan will make this much better. It isn't perfect, but in

45:32.606 --> 45:35.846
many places it is great and it makes me feel like a valued

45:35.878 --> 45:39.542
member of society when I use it. This focus on

45:39.566 --> 45:43.302
dignity is reflected in the ridership that I see when using the grt,

45:43.366 --> 45:46.646
which is refreshingly varied. It makes me proud

45:46.678 --> 45:50.450
to live here. There are people from all different backgrounds using

45:50.750 --> 45:53.926
transit in Waterloo region, which is not true in other cities

45:53.958 --> 45:56.450
in Canada when I've ridden transit there.

45:59.040 --> 46:02.744
Dignified alternatives to car use is how we induce

46:02.792 --> 46:06.632
enough usage to keep our existing road capacity open for those who

46:06.656 --> 46:09.832
need to use an automobile as we grow larger and

46:09.856 --> 46:13.672
larger as a region. I know there are a few reasons why Stage two

46:13.696 --> 46:17.416
ION isn't a part of this plan. In some is it was

46:17.488 --> 46:21.336
considered out of scope, but it remains true that we haven't

46:21.368 --> 46:25.298
heard a lot about stage 2 ion in a while and I wanted

46:25.314 --> 46:28.190
to make sure it stays on the radar. Thank you very much for your time.

46:28.730 --> 46:32.750
Thank you so much Matthew. I see no questions from council. Thank you

46:33.290 --> 46:36.482
Chair Redmond. Thank you. I'm just a little slow off the mark. I'm just

46:36.506 --> 46:40.470
wondering if Commissioner Spooner might speak to the light rail

46:41.130 --> 46:44.674
phase two. I too am a huge fan and he can maybe give you an

46:44.682 --> 46:48.210
update on the timing through your chair.

46:48.250 --> 46:52.002
And thank you for the question. Yeah, so it wasn't an

46:52.026 --> 46:54.834
omission in that It's a project still in development.

46:54.922 --> 46:58.558
And so the, the timeline and the immediacy is we've got an initial business

46:58.614 --> 47:02.462
case underway looking at a number of options to deliver rapid transit to Cambridge that

47:02.486 --> 47:06.126
will be here in November at Regional Council to

47:06.198 --> 47:09.454
advance and select an option and then we'll get into more of

47:09.462 --> 47:12.526
the detailed planning and the work. So, you know,

47:12.598 --> 47:15.870
we will add it to the map at the appropriate time. But yeah,

47:15.950 --> 47:19.182
it is running on a bit of a separate project timeline, but one with some

47:19.206 --> 47:22.968
activity very soon. Thank you. That's fantastic to hear. Thank you very much.

47:23.104 --> 47:26.460
Thank you and thanks for coming in again, Matthew.

47:26.880 --> 47:31.260
Next we have Sam Gon Salves Horton.

47:31.760 --> 47:34.220
Perfect. You've got five minutes, Sam.

47:38.480 --> 47:40.700
Okay. Is this thing on? It is. Perfect.

47:41.680 --> 47:44.856
Good evening, Regional Council. My name is Sam Gonsalves Horton.

47:44.888 --> 47:48.278
I'm here tonight to speak in strong support of the new proposed

47:48.384 --> 47:52.234
GRT business plan. I come before you tonight wearing many hats,

47:52.362 --> 47:55.738
and each one reflecting a different way that I experience and care about public transit

47:55.754 --> 47:59.482
in our region. So first and foremost, above anything else, I am a

47:59.506 --> 48:02.506
transit rider. I do not own a car. I really.

48:02.578 --> 48:06.602
I rely on the GRT for everything. Getting to class, getting my co op,

48:06.706 --> 48:10.122
grocery stores, medical appointments, meeting up with friends when

48:10.146 --> 48:13.642
buses don't run often or don't connect where they need to. It's now not

48:13.666 --> 48:17.192
just inconvenient, but it's isolating. Luckily for me, I have

48:17.216 --> 48:20.808
the privilege of living in an area of Waterloo that provides excellent service, that takes

48:20.824 --> 48:23.720
me where I want to go, when I want to go, and allows me,

48:23.760 --> 48:28.200
at least on weekdays, to spontaneously change my plans for

48:28.240 --> 48:31.672
transit riders. Every time a bus leaves without you, it's like a wall going up

48:31.696 --> 48:35.096
at the end of your laneway. If you drove a car, this would surely drive

48:35.128 --> 48:38.712
you around the bend. Right now, especially on the weekend with the

48:38.736 --> 48:42.056
12 and the two or two bus near my apartment, feels like that wall stays

48:42.088 --> 48:45.084
up just a little too long for me to be able to consistently rely on

48:45.092 --> 48:47.884
this service on the weekend, limiting the places I can go and the things I

48:47.892 --> 48:51.132
can do. This new business plan recognizes that reality

48:51.196 --> 48:54.812
and takes steps towards addressing it. Increase frequency,

48:54.876 --> 48:58.380
span and consistency. These are all things that will give people the

48:58.420 --> 49:01.000
freedom to worry less and to do more.

49:01.700 --> 49:05.560
Secondly, I'm here on behalf of tritag, the Tri Cities Transport Action Group.

49:05.940 --> 49:09.244
As Michael said, we advocate for better transit, walking and cycling infrastructure

49:09.292 --> 49:13.120
because we believe our cities work better when everyone can get around with dignity.

49:13.570 --> 49:16.842
From a regional perspective, this plan helps us move in that

49:16.866 --> 49:20.234
direction while saving on Costly road expansions. However, as Michael and others have

49:20.242 --> 49:23.834
already pointed out, being more ambitious with timelines is very, very important.

49:24.002 --> 49:27.594
I think we can get this 10 year plan down a little bit

49:27.762 --> 49:31.482
if we can five year plan down a little bit. And reducing

49:31.546 --> 49:35.034
again. Wrapped advertising on buses I think is essential to the image of transit that

49:35.042 --> 49:37.990
we're trying to cultivate here as a first class service.

49:38.830 --> 49:42.966
I'm also here as a student representative, as it was a spokesperson and UPAS

49:42.998 --> 49:46.822
Burner won't be speaking on behalf of ARIA tonight, but on behalf of

49:46.926 --> 49:50.534
our president Nick, who sadly couldn't make it. The University of

49:50.542 --> 49:54.006
Waterloo student population again is one of the largest and most consistent

49:54.038 --> 49:57.766
ridership bases in the region. Thousands of students rely on transit

49:57.798 --> 50:01.366
every day, not just for campus commutes, but to live full lives off campus

50:01.398 --> 50:04.982
as well. UW data, just to add a bit of an

50:05.006 --> 50:09.044
extra layer here, suggests that low ion

50:09.092 --> 50:13.076
and bus frequencies are the main reason why employees and students choose

50:13.108 --> 50:16.468
to drive, overtaking public transportation. The proposed

50:16.564 --> 50:20.500
improvements to evening and weekend service directly address one of the biggest concerns also

50:20.540 --> 50:23.668
that we hear from students. Buses don't run late enough or frequently enough

50:23.724 --> 50:27.140
to make us feel safe supported, especially for those working part time

50:27.180 --> 50:30.548
jobs or studying late at night on campus. Better service isn't

50:30.564 --> 50:34.350
just about convenience. It's about equity, safety and inclusion.

50:35.170 --> 50:38.778
Finally, I speak to you as an urban planning student, as somebody who cares and

50:38.834 --> 50:42.522
studies deeply about public transportation and how cities grow.

50:42.706 --> 50:45.754
When we invest in public transit, we shape a region that is more sustainable,

50:45.802 --> 50:49.242
more affordable, and more resilient. This business plan is not just

50:49.266 --> 50:52.634
a transportation document, it's a climate action plan. As Bruno said,

50:52.802 --> 50:55.910
it's a growth management strategy as well. It's an equity policy

50:56.290 --> 51:00.162
because when we make transit reliable and accessible, we reduce car dependency,

51:00.266 --> 51:04.066
we lower household costs, and we allow for more compact and complete

51:04.098 --> 51:07.762
communities to flourish. This plan represents a vision, one where

51:07.786 --> 51:11.554
the region recognizes that people deserve to have choices in how they move.

51:11.722 --> 51:15.378
The transit isn't just for those who have no other option, it's for everyone.

51:15.514 --> 51:18.242
If we want ridership to grow, if we want to hit our climate goals,

51:18.306 --> 51:21.602
if we want to increase freedom of choice and give people the opportunity to flourish,

51:21.666 --> 51:24.802
then we must be bold. And this is the kind of boldness that we need

51:24.826 --> 51:28.172
from this plan. So on behalf of myself, Tritag,

51:28.236 --> 51:31.532
WUSA and you Waterloo students, I urge you to adopt and

51:31.556 --> 51:34.812
fund this business plan. Let's continue to build a region where we

51:34.836 --> 51:38.252
put people and possibilities of what we could do first.

51:38.356 --> 51:42.140
Thank you. Excellent. Thank you, Sam. Thank you. For coming in. I see no

51:42.180 --> 51:46.620
questions from councillors. We will now move on to Rajvir

51:46.700 --> 51:48.960
Saml or Saml.

51:53.870 --> 51:57.606
Good evening Madam Chair, the council members

51:57.678 --> 52:01.142
and the staff. Thanks for giving me the

52:01.166 --> 52:05.254
opportunity to talk about the transit

52:05.302 --> 52:09.530
coverage and Huron South. I'd like to share my screen,

52:10.750 --> 52:14.130
wonder if it's visible there.

52:17.560 --> 52:20.960
Oh we have your photos that we can share

52:21.000 --> 52:23.140
here as well. Okay, perfect. Yeah.

52:24.440 --> 52:27.260
So I would like to bring that.

52:28.760 --> 52:32.240
We don't have any transit coverage in

52:32.280 --> 52:35.660
Huron on the south of here on road.

52:36.280 --> 52:40.112
If you can go to the next screen. The.

52:40.216 --> 52:43.820
The closest bus stop is about

52:44.720 --> 52:48.968
15 to 20 minute walk depending on which bus

52:49.024 --> 52:52.264
route you take. There are a couple proposals

52:52.312 --> 52:55.432
that I would like to present here.

52:55.536 --> 52:58.712
If. If I can

52:58.736 --> 53:02.540
go to the page

53:03.200 --> 53:07.800
one behind there is a existing transit route at

53:07.840 --> 53:11.776
the beginning there. If I can just quickly for my reference.

53:11.968 --> 53:14.800
So everybody. Okay,

53:14.960 --> 53:18.416
maybe that got missed. Anyways, I can just start

53:18.488 --> 53:22.048
on the next page. With option one we. We get

53:22.104 --> 53:26.352
transit where this roundabout is, that's the closest transport

53:26.496 --> 53:30.608
stop we have. And then there's another one

53:30.744 --> 53:33.984
on the roundabout of Starsburg Road and Huron

53:34.032 --> 53:38.100
Road. And this area here

53:38.140 --> 53:41.492
where I'm proposing the new route if the transit

53:41.556 --> 53:45.508
can accommodate that would give access to all

53:45.564 --> 53:48.080
of the housing on the south of Huron Road.

53:49.260 --> 53:53.284
And this area is growing pretty fast,

53:53.332 --> 53:56.852
like we're building new houses there. There's a community center

53:56.916 --> 53:59.560
which has no access to transit.

54:00.700 --> 54:03.172
We have multiplexes, condos,

54:03.236 --> 54:06.424
townhomes. And if you can reduce the car traffic

54:06.472 --> 54:10.152
there it would resolve the parking issues as well. Because I see

54:10.176 --> 54:13.384
a lot of cars parked on the roads. I see people walking

54:13.432 --> 54:17.048
and in the winter you can just imagine taking

54:17.104 --> 54:20.712
a walk which is 15 minutes and you have

54:20.736 --> 54:23.380
a negative 20 degrees and it's snowing or.

54:24.880 --> 54:29.000
And. And the other thing I would like to point out is where the Brigadoon

54:29.080 --> 54:32.120
woods are. That area has a lot of

54:32.160 --> 54:36.440
wildlife so you can encounter coyotes

54:36.520 --> 54:39.992
and raccoons and I've seen foxes there. So just

54:40.016 --> 54:43.320
imagine you were just walking by yourself and you

54:43.360 --> 54:47.540
encountered a wild animal. And I mean that's kind of a

54:48.080 --> 54:52.152
situation that people are facing there. And this proposed

54:52.216 --> 54:56.386
route has paved concrete which

54:56.458 --> 54:59.590
was designed for transit route.

55:00.170 --> 55:04.002
So at some point there was a plan to

55:04.026 --> 55:07.346
bring transit there but I believe it hasn't happened

55:07.378 --> 55:11.090
yet. So if things could move on

55:11.130 --> 55:15.346
quick and we can have transit because

55:15.498 --> 55:19.106
the houses are going up. I think the community

55:19.178 --> 55:23.840
has grown like last six years. I've been living there since 2019

55:25.180 --> 55:28.644
and I've seen the area grow quite a bit.

55:28.812 --> 55:33.012
We have got the Plazas and everything. So if we

55:33.036 --> 55:37.396
can get better connectivity, that would be great. And with the

55:37.548 --> 55:41.220
condos, townhouses, there are a lot of people who are renting them

55:41.260 --> 55:45.316
out. There are a lot of students. Due to the closeness of Conestoga

55:45.348 --> 55:48.964
College, we do get a lot of

55:49.132 --> 55:52.884
international students there who are using transit as their

55:52.972 --> 55:56.976
main accommodation. And just carrying a grocery bag

55:57.168 --> 56:00.576
and walking for 10 minutes, I think it

56:00.648 --> 56:04.420
just becomes challenging.

56:05.080 --> 56:09.152
So that's all that I would like to request

56:09.296 --> 56:11.820
that if something could be done about it,

56:13.480 --> 56:17.504
that would be really appreciated. Thank you.

56:17.672 --> 56:21.648
Rashvir. We've spoken about this and communicated via email.

56:21.704 --> 56:24.722
I do want staff, I think, Kevin, you're, you're going to respond and then we

56:24.746 --> 56:28.194
have a couple questions from Councillor. So hang on the line there.

56:28.282 --> 56:29.350
Go ahead, Kevin,

56:31.930 --> 56:35.618
through the chair. Thank you very much, Rajvir, for taking the time to speak

56:35.634 --> 56:38.402
to us tonight. And I think one of the things we're excited about working with

56:38.426 --> 56:41.778
our local municipal partners. When we've thought about planning out

56:41.834 --> 56:45.070
for our network and strategic new coverage areas.

56:45.770 --> 56:49.382
You know, Southwest Kitchener has been a key, a key priority for us

56:49.406 --> 56:53.090
when we consider new coverage areas. The plan

56:53.710 --> 56:57.542
really anchors on some improvements for us to connect in the

56:57.566 --> 57:01.890
Wollaston area and then we have some proposals for, for new coverage in

57:02.270 --> 57:05.622
early to middle years of the, of our, of our plan today.

57:05.726 --> 57:08.774
So one of the big pieces we will be balancing is,

57:08.862 --> 57:12.294
is that building out the frequent transit network. We've heard a

57:12.302 --> 57:15.712
lot here today and still bringing forward for consideration

57:15.776 --> 57:19.180
some options for new coverage areas as well too.

57:19.720 --> 57:23.328
So yeah, this is one piece. We hear you and,

57:23.384 --> 57:26.928
and future options will be presented for, for sort

57:26.944 --> 57:31.024
of refined feedback on a couple of different options in this area. Thank you.

57:31.192 --> 57:34.416
Yeah, thanks. Okay, thank you, Kevin.

57:34.608 --> 57:38.464
Councilor Hune. Thank you, Chair James, and thank you to the delegate

57:38.512 --> 57:41.850
for advocating for transit in your area.

57:42.550 --> 57:45.966
We are aware of many transit deserts, unfortunately throughout

57:45.998 --> 57:49.198
our region and we are addressing those as best we can.

57:49.254 --> 57:52.782
But Commissioner Spooner, I wonder if you would like to speak

57:52.846 --> 57:56.766
to how we approach transit

57:56.798 --> 57:59.890
deserts and decide on new routes, et cetera, et cetera.

58:00.710 --> 58:04.334
Through you, Chair. Thank you, Councillor Huneck. I think you know, this is one of

58:04.342 --> 58:08.136
the challenging areas for us when we approach plan and budget every year.

58:08.288 --> 58:11.672
We've got two needs that we need to address. One of them is

58:11.776 --> 58:15.112
we're a really fast growing region and so we've got new

58:15.216 --> 58:18.536
areas emerging that need service. This is our geographic

58:18.568 --> 58:21.752
expansion, our service expansion need. The other thing that we

58:21.776 --> 58:25.256
have is we have a lot of ridership and people who are interested in riding

58:25.288 --> 58:28.760
existing routes. And so we also have to address crowding,

58:28.920 --> 58:32.680
reliability and on time performance. And so the reason that

58:32.720 --> 58:35.980
we didn't put forward an implementation plan as part of this

58:36.020 --> 58:39.820
business plan is that we don't guess. And so what

58:39.860 --> 58:43.692
we'll look to do is through each year monitor ridership, monitor where that is

58:43.716 --> 58:47.692
happening in routes, and bring forward to council a

58:47.716 --> 58:51.164
transit service plan for the following year that looks to achieve all of these various

58:51.212 --> 58:54.876
goals. So as Kevin noted, we've got a lot of neighborhoods who

58:54.948 --> 58:58.316
really worked with us through the development of this plan. There is a map

58:58.348 --> 59:02.124
in one of the attachments that was with the plan that does show a lot

59:02.132 --> 59:05.870
of that geographic expansion. We're excited to get there and welcome

59:05.950 --> 59:09.278
new neighborhoods. And it is critical that we get there early.

59:09.414 --> 59:12.798
People make decisions about their travel habits early on

59:12.854 --> 59:16.302
after moving. If there's a life event, a new job, a new place to

59:16.326 --> 59:19.902
live, starting university, those are the times when people make

59:19.926 --> 59:23.646
travel decisions. And so making sure that we're ready with service as our community grows

59:23.758 --> 59:27.102
is one of the priorities identified in the plan. So thank you to the

59:27.126 --> 59:30.850
delegate for bringing that forward. Thank you. And chair Redmond.

59:31.590 --> 59:35.422
Thank you, Chair James. I just want to thank this delegate and the

59:35.446 --> 59:38.830
other delegations that came bringing suggestions. They've been very

59:38.870 --> 59:42.158
thoughtful in their interventions. Clearly they've read the plan and they've

59:42.174 --> 59:45.822
given us really valuable feedback. So this has been a really interesting

59:45.886 --> 59:49.262
session. I know it's not over, but I just wanted to say thank you for

59:49.286 --> 59:52.650
all the positivity that you brought to this process.

59:54.070 --> 59:57.654
Thank you. Councilor Rodriguez, I saw your name in the queue. Are you. You're good?

59:57.782 --> 01:00:00.838
Okay, thank you. Okay, thank you so much.

01:00:00.894 --> 01:00:04.214
Rajvir. And next we have Barry

01:00:04.262 --> 01:00:06.610
Green. Believe you're in person.

01:00:18.030 --> 01:00:21.670
Hi. I just wanted to come and talk to you about. I support you guys.

01:00:21.750 --> 01:00:25.462
Transit plants are improvements, but I

01:00:25.486 --> 01:00:29.526
like to see Cambridge is so far when it comes to transit

01:00:29.558 --> 01:00:32.742
improvements. We don't have like kitchen and

01:00:32.766 --> 01:00:35.970
Waterloo has early bus service and everything else and

01:00:36.510 --> 01:00:40.086
late night service. We have a nice gaslight district downtown

01:00:40.198 --> 01:00:43.846
and if you want to go to stuff like that, when you taking

01:00:43.918 --> 01:00:47.460
a bus, some of our buses stop like 11:30,

01:00:47.653 --> 01:00:50.896
12:00 and so you can't even go down downtown

01:00:50.928 --> 01:00:54.464
where they, they spent all this money to get this dash light district going.

01:00:54.632 --> 01:00:58.352
And we don't have late night bus service. And also when

01:00:58.376 --> 01:01:01.280
it comes to Sunday service buses,

01:01:01.440 --> 01:01:05.520
I work at Kitchener, Costco and sometimes with

01:01:05.560 --> 01:01:08.944
our shifts, I don't know if you've ever said I'm Costco. But I work at

01:01:08.952 --> 01:01:12.464
the front door at Costco and we open up at 9 o'clock in

01:01:12.472 --> 01:01:15.920
the morning and some of our shifts start at 8:00,

01:01:16.000 --> 01:01:19.738
sorry, 8:45 in the morning. And when

01:01:19.794 --> 01:01:23.578
buses don't run early enough on Sunday service, because I

01:01:23.634 --> 01:01:26.634
checked the schedules and for my area,

01:01:26.802 --> 01:01:30.874
I'm over at Myers Road and cheese

01:01:30.922 --> 01:01:34.986
factory area, our buses don't start until 8:30

01:01:35.058 --> 01:01:38.794
in the morning either way going up to the mall or

01:01:38.882 --> 01:01:43.402
downtown. And when you and I get to work for 8:45

01:01:43.586 --> 01:01:47.278
in the morning, you gotta take a taxi in. And that's like

01:01:47.334 --> 01:01:50.590
$40 one way. So that's like

01:01:50.630 --> 01:01:54.158
two hours of my schedule, of my

01:01:54.214 --> 01:01:57.390
time just to pay for a taxi.

01:01:57.470 --> 01:02:01.054
Like it's nice there, it's okay getting home and everything else.

01:02:01.102 --> 01:02:04.494
But when you gotta get to work in the morning, they need

01:02:04.662 --> 01:02:08.318
earlier transit times. Like it's like even before

01:02:08.374 --> 01:02:11.726
regional transit came involved, Kitchener had earlier bus

01:02:11.798 --> 01:02:15.236
times. But us in Cambridge, we always

01:02:15.308 --> 01:02:19.200
seem to be the ones that are not getting the service that we need

01:02:19.740 --> 01:02:23.092
and all that. So I would

01:02:23.116 --> 01:02:26.804
like to see Cambridge be kind of top prioritized

01:02:26.852 --> 01:02:30.292
with all these new improvements just so people can

01:02:30.316 --> 01:02:34.468
get out and enjoy themselves. Like always have people say

01:02:34.604 --> 01:02:38.386
that, okay, let's go downtown, let's go to the Gaslight district.

01:02:38.548 --> 01:02:42.118
We have all these bands going on or some nights

01:02:42.134 --> 01:02:46.342
when they do movies or show

01:02:46.446 --> 01:02:49.734
sport games there. Well, if you want to stay to the end

01:02:49.742 --> 01:02:53.462
of the event, you can't because all the trans,

01:02:53.566 --> 01:02:56.918
all the buses have stopped and all that. So you're

01:02:56.934 --> 01:03:00.262
kind of like, okay, what can I do? Where can I

01:03:00.286 --> 01:03:04.902
go? It's kind of limited. When we don't have the

01:03:04.926 --> 01:03:08.230
bus service, they can't everywhere. Because now since

01:03:08.270 --> 01:03:11.574
they raised the way, I think quote, notice that

01:03:11.662 --> 01:03:15.590
since they raised a fee, they considered a taxi. Before, it used to be

01:03:15.630 --> 01:03:19.062
zero. Now it's like five or whatever dollars is to sit in

01:03:19.086 --> 01:03:22.486
a taxi. And it does add up, especially when you're

01:03:22.518 --> 01:03:25.782
on fixed income and you rely on transit.

01:03:25.926 --> 01:03:29.526
It's nice having that transit program.

01:03:29.598 --> 01:03:33.490
It does help pay for our bus passes. But when you

01:03:33.530 --> 01:03:36.946
want to get out and about and you can't get taxi.

01:03:37.058 --> 01:03:40.130
I have a learning disability because I had meningitis at three

01:03:40.170 --> 01:03:43.010
months of age. And when you can't get mobility,

01:03:43.090 --> 01:03:46.630
plus because you don't fall under criteria,

01:03:47.370 --> 01:03:50.722
it's hard to get out in the boat and do things. So I

01:03:50.746 --> 01:03:54.402
just thought I'd come and stress my opinion that it

01:03:54.426 --> 01:03:58.882
would be nice to see that Cambridge, when it comes to the improvements

01:03:58.946 --> 01:04:02.414
that Cambridge does get some kind of service

01:04:02.582 --> 01:04:05.918
early morning especially. And all that for people that do

01:04:05.974 --> 01:04:09.838
work in different areas that can't get there by

01:04:09.974 --> 01:04:12.970
bus. By bus service early in the morning.

01:04:13.670 --> 01:04:16.590
Thank you. Thank you so much,

01:04:16.710 --> 01:04:18.490
Kevin, for coming in.

01:04:20.150 --> 01:04:24.290
We do have a few questions from some counselors. Councilor Wolf,

01:04:24.870 --> 01:04:28.490
through you, Madam Chair, and Barry, thank you so much for coming.

01:04:29.040 --> 01:04:32.984
I know you said you read the letter in the

01:04:32.992 --> 01:04:36.936
paper where I was complaining and I'm glad that you've

01:04:36.968 --> 01:04:41.256
come to also share your problems

01:04:41.288 --> 01:04:44.696
that you're having. How long does it take you to get,

01:04:44.768 --> 01:04:48.312
when you do get the bus to go to work from where you

01:04:48.336 --> 01:04:52.168
live, it usually takes 45 minutes

01:04:52.344 --> 01:04:56.018
to an hour. It all really depends on connection wise

01:04:56.114 --> 01:05:00.110
and, and just transit times and, and all that.

01:05:00.490 --> 01:05:03.842
And like, no, that would be nice to have

01:05:03.866 --> 01:05:07.682
like better service that area. And I know this is not the place to

01:05:07.706 --> 01:05:10.830
say it, but there's a lot of transit people here

01:05:11.530 --> 01:05:15.410
at Kitchen. At Costco, we do have a lot of people that do

01:05:15.450 --> 01:05:18.882
take the bus. It would be nice to have a bus shelter

01:05:18.946 --> 01:05:22.940
out that way because when you're waiting out there.

01:05:23.060 --> 01:05:26.540
I don't know if asked for this earlier, but if you brought up the

01:05:26.580 --> 01:05:29.676
graph of that picture of that area, there is really

01:05:29.748 --> 01:05:33.420
no shelters when you're coming up towards

01:05:33.500 --> 01:05:37.052
that area. There's one by the,

01:05:37.236 --> 01:05:42.060
across from the old theater jump zone and

01:05:42.100 --> 01:05:45.708
all that. But I hardly ever see anybody use

01:05:45.764 --> 01:05:49.140
that shelter because of that movie theater not being there

01:05:49.180 --> 01:05:52.548
anymore. So that shelter is just sitting vacant, not being

01:05:52.604 --> 01:05:56.340
used. When I think that if

01:05:56.380 --> 01:05:59.732
we look at moving that shelter in

01:05:59.756 --> 01:06:03.600
front of Costco or whatever just somewhere along that way

01:06:03.980 --> 01:06:07.860
so we could have a

01:06:07.900 --> 01:06:11.668
shelter and then I said

01:06:11.724 --> 01:06:15.076
rocks up in an area. But when it comes to bus stops

01:06:15.108 --> 01:06:18.900
along there, because I work early mornings and if I

01:06:18.940 --> 01:06:22.468
had to get off at Gateway and King there

01:06:22.524 --> 01:06:25.760
by the,

01:06:26.380 --> 01:06:29.572
sorry, the store there, I had

01:06:29.596 --> 01:06:33.556
to walk back like half a block to where the

01:06:33.708 --> 01:06:36.852
Home Depot parking lot is because I had to go in the

01:06:36.876 --> 01:06:40.548
back way. I know a long time ago they had an extra bus stop

01:06:40.604 --> 01:06:43.720
along there, but they removed it because of,

01:06:43.840 --> 01:06:47.208
I guess it was too close to the other one. But it would be nice

01:06:47.224 --> 01:06:51.144
to have one there. So when people have to work early morning at Costco,

01:06:51.272 --> 01:06:55.112
they don't have to get off at the lights and walk back just to go

01:06:55.136 --> 01:07:00.300
into work because we can't go in the front doors until after 9:00.

01:07:02.800 --> 01:07:06.060
Thank you very much. Thank you, Chair Redmond.

01:07:07.130 --> 01:07:10.850
Thank you, Chair James. Thank you for coming in. I'm wondering If Commissioner Spooner can

01:07:10.890 --> 01:07:14.402
just maybe address. You brought up something an earlier delegation

01:07:14.466 --> 01:07:17.810
talked about advocating for late night buses. And what I hear

01:07:17.850 --> 01:07:20.866
you saying is that you're looking at late night and early bus service.

01:07:20.938 --> 01:07:24.434
I'm just wondering if Commissioner Spooner could talk to a little bit about the process,

01:07:24.522 --> 01:07:28.514
how we decide when we run buses and how those schedules

01:07:28.562 --> 01:07:33.132
are formed through

01:07:33.156 --> 01:07:36.120
you, Chair. And thank you, Chair, for the question.

01:07:36.740 --> 01:07:40.604
Barry, I think you've given a really good presentation on the human side of transit

01:07:40.652 --> 01:07:43.804
today and I'm excited to respond because you've hit a lot of

01:07:43.812 --> 01:07:46.908
the main elements of the bus and train plan. So let's run through it.

01:07:47.044 --> 01:07:50.476
You talked about weekdays and weekends, frequent transit network addresses

01:07:50.508 --> 01:07:54.140
that you talked about riding in the same journey on your way

01:07:54.180 --> 01:07:57.532
to and from work. And that's what consistent schedules are intended to get

01:07:57.556 --> 01:08:01.322
out. You talked about the opportunity to have a good time

01:08:01.346 --> 01:08:05.590
in the Gaslight district. And that's what the overnight network gets at.

01:08:06.050 --> 01:08:09.530
And the one piece that you didn't, but I would invite everybody to come along

01:08:09.570 --> 01:08:13.034
with you to the Gaslight region is talking about the Highway Express network and the

01:08:13.042 --> 01:08:17.306
ability to move quickly between Kitchener Waterloo and Cambridge. So I think you've

01:08:17.338 --> 01:08:20.650
really hit all of the main elements that we're trying to address with this plan.

01:08:20.690 --> 01:08:24.762
And I see tremendous benefit to you to

01:08:24.786 --> 01:08:27.350
the specific question on how do we decide when.

01:08:27.890 --> 01:08:31.306
Again, it's data driven, it's based off ridership,

01:08:31.498 --> 01:08:34.602
but it's also sometimes based on the limitations of

01:08:34.626 --> 01:08:38.122
our service and the resources we have. And so when we look at those opportunities,

01:08:38.186 --> 01:08:41.930
the reason that you're seeing consistent schedules that addresses that

01:08:41.970 --> 01:08:45.242
consistent trip, whether early in the morning or later in the day, we see

01:08:45.266 --> 01:08:48.634
that as a major economic driver that provides businesses in

01:08:48.642 --> 01:08:51.770
our community the opportunity to set up shifts in that way knowing that their workers

01:08:51.810 --> 01:08:56.729
can get there. And the second piece to that was

01:08:57.909 --> 01:09:00.973
the overnight piece, which again might offer the opportunity

01:09:01.021 --> 01:09:04.589
for triple shift. And so we've got to get into all of this plan.

01:09:04.709 --> 01:09:07.421
I'm excited for you, Barry. I think this is about to make things real good

01:09:07.445 --> 01:09:11.053
for you. But again, thank you for putting that human element onto the

01:09:11.061 --> 01:09:15.213
business plan. But my point is that it seems like when it comes to improvements,

01:09:15.341 --> 01:09:18.893
keywords is always usually the last person,

01:09:18.981 --> 01:09:22.866
last bet to get and all that. Like, we just need to get ours

01:09:22.978 --> 01:09:26.642
like up to par like what Kitchener has. Because when you got to work an

01:09:26.666 --> 01:09:29.550
early morning shift, you have to try to.

01:09:30.090 --> 01:09:33.602
I'm not trying to say anybody here, but when it comes to we have an

01:09:33.626 --> 01:09:37.378
early morning Shift. And sometimes you'd

01:09:37.394 --> 01:09:40.722
say you don't have the cash on you and everything else you got to

01:09:40.746 --> 01:09:44.002
say, okay, sorry, can I start at 9 or whatever because

01:09:44.026 --> 01:09:47.162
I can't get in. But then you're kind of the. It doesn't look good on

01:09:47.186 --> 01:09:50.682
you, your part that you have to give away a

01:09:50.706 --> 01:09:54.362
shift or whatever because you can't get in. And it would

01:09:54.386 --> 01:09:57.830
be nice because I do have this ability, but just from

01:09:58.290 --> 01:10:01.818
learning disability, I don't fall under that criteria

01:10:01.914 --> 01:10:05.226
for Mobility Plus. So if I had Mobility plus

01:10:05.338 --> 01:10:08.490
on those days, on a Sunday where I have to work

01:10:08.530 --> 01:10:11.744
early, I can just book ahead and say, okay,

01:10:11.842 --> 01:10:13.960
Mobility plus and everything else.

01:10:16.140 --> 01:10:19.668
Thank you. One more question, Barry, from Councillor Huneck.

01:10:19.764 --> 01:10:23.572
Thank you, Chair James, and thank you, Barry, for sharing very vulnerably that

01:10:23.596 --> 01:10:26.916
it costs you $40 to get into work when the buses

01:10:26.948 --> 01:10:30.564
don't run. I think that's very important for regional council to

01:10:30.572 --> 01:10:34.164
be aware of in situations

01:10:34.212 --> 01:10:37.934
where someone doesn't qualify for Mobility plus

01:10:38.092 --> 01:10:41.978
and the buses aren't running. Commissioner Spinner, is there a flex bus

01:10:42.034 --> 01:10:45.498
route or a Bus plus route that

01:10:45.634 --> 01:10:49.350
could fill that gap? Do you,

01:10:51.570 --> 01:10:55.082
through your chair? Good question, Councillor Huenig. So we've been looking at, as part

01:10:55.106 --> 01:10:58.474
of the Night Network development, the overnight

01:10:58.522 --> 01:11:01.590
network, what that could look like. And so

01:11:01.890 --> 01:11:05.274
that can expand out of the Night Network into other times of day and

01:11:05.362 --> 01:11:09.010
weekdays and weekends. But what we've been looking at is that

01:11:09.050 --> 01:11:12.834
Night Network would look a lot like the existing I Express

01:11:12.922 --> 01:11:15.570
network. And then the question becomes,

01:11:15.730 --> 01:11:19.522
how do you manage the first and last mile? So, you know, we do have,

01:11:19.706 --> 01:11:23.154
you know, places to go that exist on that network, but we

01:11:23.162 --> 01:11:26.642
also have many places to go and work that do not. And so we have

01:11:26.666 --> 01:11:30.082
been exploring, we've run an on demand pilot, Route 79 to the airport. We've had

01:11:30.106 --> 01:11:34.082
some great learnings out of that. And so we are exploring how

01:11:34.106 --> 01:11:37.522
to deliver a bit more of that kind of personalized service at different times of

01:11:37.546 --> 01:11:40.834
day using different vehicle types. So it is mentioned in

01:11:40.842 --> 01:11:43.202
the plan in the overnight, but I think if we learn lessons from that and

01:11:43.226 --> 01:11:46.642
we can make it work effectively, we can use it in other times of day.

01:11:46.746 --> 01:11:50.750
And just to follow up, sorry, Chair James, if someone

01:11:51.210 --> 01:11:54.690
didn't qualify for Mobility plus

01:11:54.770 --> 01:12:01.500
but thinks they could, should they apply again through

01:12:01.540 --> 01:12:05.052
your chair? Yeah, I think so. Councillor Huneck, you and I

01:12:05.076 --> 01:12:09.084
have talked about this. We have the nature of an accessibility requirement

01:12:09.132 --> 01:12:12.252
is that sometimes they are fixed and sometimes they are a bit more dynamic and

01:12:12.276 --> 01:12:15.596
they change and they change over time. And so, yeah,

01:12:15.628 --> 01:12:18.684
Barry, I'd be happy if you wanted to have a chat with somebody from my

01:12:18.692 --> 01:12:21.692
team or you wanted to reapply to see if that was an option for you.

01:12:21.716 --> 01:12:25.490
But yes, Councillor Huneck, we do accept. Repeat there. There's no issue with

01:12:25.530 --> 01:12:29.058
somebody trying again. Thank you.

01:12:29.114 --> 01:12:32.402
Thank you. Barry, do you want to respond? I just want to

01:12:32.426 --> 01:12:36.498
say I know I've read up on all the routes that are getting the overnight

01:12:36.514 --> 01:12:40.290
and late night service, but when it comes to accessing it from

01:12:40.330 --> 01:12:44.270
my area, it does not apply to my area.

01:12:44.890 --> 01:12:48.450
So I like the idea of late

01:12:48.490 --> 01:12:51.790
night early morning bus service. But. But my area,

01:12:52.330 --> 01:12:56.030
I don't fall under the criteria of having that

01:12:56.570 --> 01:13:00.498
because I take the 53 or the 50 and

01:13:00.634 --> 01:13:04.146
just to get downtown or to the mall. I don't. My buses don't

01:13:04.178 --> 01:13:07.634
even match up with any of that new info that's going

01:13:07.642 --> 01:13:11.250
to be coming for all the new routes. I looked into that

01:13:11.290 --> 01:13:15.138
and there's only like the 206 and the 51

01:13:15.194 --> 01:13:19.130
and 300 whatever. But my buses in my area

01:13:20.310 --> 01:13:23.870
for that side of the cult do not match up with

01:13:23.990 --> 01:13:27.902
those routes that's getting that improvement. So I decided to put it

01:13:27.926 --> 01:13:31.166
out there. That would be nice to see some of the other routes instead

01:13:31.198 --> 01:13:34.526
of just the mainline one. What about the arterial ones

01:13:34.558 --> 01:13:38.526
that get people to the main lines? What about their routes?

01:13:38.718 --> 01:13:41.850
They're saying thank you. Thank you,

01:13:42.790 --> 01:13:46.222
Doug. Maybe it's worth a follow up with Barry after.

01:13:46.326 --> 01:13:49.170
Okay, thank you Barry.

01:13:49.510 --> 01:13:54.210
Now moving on to Kevin then Dunnan.

01:13:54.550 --> 01:13:56.610
Perfect. You have five minutes, Kevin.

01:13:58.150 --> 01:14:01.902
Thanks everybody for the opportunity to speak tonight. I want to speak briefly on my

01:14:01.926 --> 01:14:05.230
support for GRT's business plan. The frequent transit network

01:14:05.270 --> 01:14:08.590
will greatly improve service to the residents of Waterloo region. Across North

01:14:08.630 --> 01:14:12.014
America, many transit agencies like Toronto's TTC report that they

01:14:12.022 --> 01:14:15.890
are experiencing the greatest ridership increases on weekends and off peak service.

01:14:16.430 --> 01:14:19.974
Our current weekend and off peak frequency for most routes of 30 minutes is limiting

01:14:20.022 --> 01:14:23.526
and will prevent GRT from this growth. I've often experienced

01:14:23.558 --> 01:14:26.854
people deciding to drive or take ride share rather than use transit on the

01:14:26.862 --> 01:14:30.486
weekend. Because waiting at a stop or for a long transit transfer is extremely

01:14:30.518 --> 01:14:34.246
inconvenient and makes a trip take much longer. The frequent transit

01:14:34.278 --> 01:14:38.374
network with 15 minute or minimum 15 minute frequency and 10 minute frequency during commute

01:14:38.422 --> 01:14:42.262
hours will make transit far more competitive with driving and ride sharing. It will also

01:14:42.286 --> 01:14:45.686
provide better access to jobs, services and leisure. With the frequent

01:14:45.718 --> 01:14:49.014
transit network, many riders will no longer feel the need to exhaustively research the

01:14:49.022 --> 01:14:51.942
best timing for their route. They can simply arrive at a stop and trust that

01:14:51.966 --> 01:14:56.166
transit will arrive soon. I'd also like to add that the Kensilian Waterloo

01:14:56.198 --> 01:14:59.942
Region museum deserves a better bus stop. I know the business plan

01:14:59.966 --> 01:15:03.750
is wider in scope than a single location, but especially with the hopeful reopening

01:15:03.830 --> 01:15:07.392
of Dune Heritage Village, our community deserves better or deserves and

01:15:07.416 --> 01:15:11.152
needs better bus access there. The current arrangement is that takes a 12

01:15:11.176 --> 01:15:14.896
minute walk to get to the museum and requires crossing a loud

01:15:14.928 --> 01:15:19.120
and dangerous boulevard with hostile traffic at intersections. This becomes more

01:15:19.240 --> 01:15:23.376
inaccessible through heavy rain or extremely hot days. I hope you'll discuss a better solution.

01:15:23.488 --> 01:15:26.800
Thank you. Thank you, Kevin. I have

01:15:26.840 --> 01:15:30.928
no questions from councilors. Next is Nathan

01:15:31.104 --> 01:15:34.234
Penita and you are online.

01:15:34.402 --> 01:15:37.962
You have five minutes, Nathan. Good evening, members of the

01:15:37.986 --> 01:15:42.026
chair, council staff and all present. My name is Nathan. I'm a lifelong resident

01:15:42.058 --> 01:15:45.514
of Kitchener. I'm currently living in a newer active development project in

01:15:45.522 --> 01:15:49.322
the city Southwest. I work as a social support worker in Waterloo and

01:15:49.346 --> 01:15:52.826
I'm also a paramedic student at Conestoga College. I appreciate the opportunity

01:15:52.858 --> 01:15:56.230
to speak with you today in support of the GRT business plan.

01:15:56.850 --> 01:16:00.154
I want to speak to the very real impact that improvements to transit would have

01:16:00.162 --> 01:16:03.076
on myself, my peers and our wider community.

01:16:03.228 --> 01:16:06.372
Like many people my age, I rely on a mix of transportation.

01:16:06.516 --> 01:16:10.372
Mainly biking, taking the bus and occasionally driving. Though I don't own my

01:16:10.396 --> 01:16:14.148
own motor vehicle. Here's the reality. To get to Dune

01:16:14.164 --> 01:16:17.796
College campus from where I live, it takes about 20 minutes by car, 40 minutes

01:16:17.828 --> 01:16:21.428
by bike and over 70 minutes by bike or sorry, by bus.

01:16:21.564 --> 01:16:25.012
That's a 50 minute difference between car and bus for a 15 kilometer

01:16:25.076 --> 01:16:28.452
trip. One of the biggest reasons for that is that my nearest bus stop is

01:16:28.476 --> 01:16:32.054
about a 40 minute walk from home. This is a common issue in expanding

01:16:32.102 --> 01:16:35.878
areas of Kitchener Waterloo. While our cities continue to grow, the public

01:16:35.934 --> 01:16:39.702
transportation network has not kept pace. Transit needs to be accessible and

01:16:39.726 --> 01:16:43.446
reliable if we want people to use it. Without frequent service, better coverage

01:16:43.478 --> 01:16:47.382
and improved scheduling, public transit simply can't compete with the convenience of

01:16:47.406 --> 01:16:50.870
a car. And most people won't consider it a real option. But this

01:16:50.910 --> 01:16:54.310
plan offers, in part, a solution. Expanding service

01:16:54.350 --> 01:16:58.036
in areas like mine, Sunrise in the Southwest means more people can access

01:16:58.108 --> 01:17:01.684
the grt. Improving frequency and schedule reliability

01:17:01.732 --> 01:17:05.524
also makes transit more attractive and competitive. The data backs this up.

01:17:05.612 --> 01:17:08.932
When transit becomes more viable, ridership increases, and when

01:17:08.956 --> 01:17:12.820
people ride more, our roads become safer, our communities more connected, and our

01:17:12.860 --> 01:17:16.676
growth more sustainable. I'm speaking not just from personal experience,

01:17:16.748 --> 01:17:19.940
but from professional and community perspectives as well. I work

01:17:19.980 --> 01:17:22.840
closely with people who don't drive for many different reasons.

01:17:23.280 --> 01:17:27.928
Age ranging from young to old due to disability, income or circumstance.

01:17:28.104 --> 01:17:31.672
Transit is a lifeline for these residents. When it doesn't reach them or when

01:17:31.696 --> 01:17:35.416
it takes too long to be practical, they're excluded from opportunities,

01:17:35.608 --> 01:17:38.872
jobs, healthcare, education and community involvement.

01:17:39.016 --> 01:17:42.088
I've also spent substantial time abroad in Denmark,

01:17:42.104 --> 01:17:45.384
where I experienced firsthand what an efficient, well connected transit

01:17:45.432 --> 01:17:48.552
system can look and feel like. I didn't drive there yet.

01:17:48.576 --> 01:17:52.436
I never felt restricted transit offered safety, comfort and independence

01:17:52.468 --> 01:17:55.908
for all. We deserve that same standard here, and this business

01:17:55.964 --> 01:17:59.092
plan takes us a step in that direction. I also want to

01:17:59.116 --> 01:18:01.892
briefly touch on safety because I think it's a critical point.

01:18:01.996 --> 01:18:05.332
As a future paramedic, I'm acutely aware of the dangers associated with

01:18:05.356 --> 01:18:09.396
car dependence. The leading cause of death for people around my age 22

01:18:09.468 --> 01:18:13.540
is unintentional injuries most often caused from motor vehicle collisions.

01:18:13.700 --> 01:18:17.060
Distracted and impaired driving are persistent public health problems.

01:18:17.220 --> 01:18:20.932
Public transit is statistically far safer and every person we can shift from

01:18:20.956 --> 01:18:24.596
a car to a bus helps reduce risk for everyone. Finally,

01:18:24.628 --> 01:18:27.572
I'd like to note that I wasn't able to attend this meeting in person due

01:18:27.596 --> 01:18:31.476
to the same issue I've spoken about lack of accessible transit in my area.

01:18:31.628 --> 01:18:35.604
The GRT just wasn't capable of lining up with my schedule today. That alone

01:18:35.652 --> 01:18:39.300
speaks volumes about the current gaps, but it's also a hopeful reminder of

01:18:39.340 --> 01:18:42.772
how this plan could change things, not just for me, but for everyone who

01:18:42.796 --> 01:18:45.812
wants a better, safer and more equitable way to get around.

01:18:45.996 --> 01:18:50.212
In summary, this business plan supports ridership, enhances community cohesion

01:18:50.276 --> 01:18:53.844
and improves public safety. I urge you to give it your full support for a

01:18:53.852 --> 01:18:57.316
healthier, more accessible and more connected Kitchener Waterloo.

01:18:57.428 --> 01:19:00.996
Thank you for your time. Thank you, Nathan. We do have a question

01:19:01.068 --> 01:19:02.840
from Councilor Rodriguez.

01:19:04.380 --> 01:19:07.796
Thank you, Nathan. And through Chair, I'm wondering if staff could comment

01:19:07.868 --> 01:19:11.844
on something that was picked up earlier around some

01:19:11.852 --> 01:19:15.460
of the investments that are shown in the business plan around Sunrise center, the Wollaston

01:19:15.540 --> 01:19:19.264
Terminal, which is shown in the plan now, and what those improvements

01:19:19.312 --> 01:19:22.864
might allow staff to do in terms of connecting folks

01:19:22.912 --> 01:19:26.560
to buses that get to different places, how they

01:19:26.600 --> 01:19:30.672
can connect from Southwest Kitchener to Conestoga College or downtown.

01:19:30.816 --> 01:19:33.780
If you could just maybe comment on that, that'd be great. Kevin,

01:19:35.400 --> 01:19:39.168
through the Chair and thank you very much for the delegation tonight. That was

01:19:39.304 --> 01:19:43.008
some really impactful commentary. I appreciate that. And to answer

01:19:43.024 --> 01:19:46.508
the inquiry, yeah, there's a couple pieces that all kind of come together

01:19:46.564 --> 01:19:49.676
when we look at both the strategic new coverage and what we can unlock with

01:19:49.748 --> 01:19:53.324
both the frequent transit network, some new coverage and even the proposed

01:19:53.372 --> 01:19:56.972
highway Express. So when we leverage the opportunity

01:19:57.036 --> 01:20:00.844
to enhance speed and look at how we can utilize our highway

01:20:00.892 --> 01:20:04.732
networks better for reducing the trip and

01:20:04.756 --> 01:20:08.000
travel times across our community from east west. For example,

01:20:08.340 --> 01:20:11.964
you know, particularly speak to the Sunrise center and propose Highway

01:20:12.012 --> 01:20:16.378
Express service to there. We can use that to unlock

01:20:16.554 --> 01:20:20.266
access across communities as sort of a jumping

01:20:20.298 --> 01:20:24.106
off point. Then in addition to that, we then overlay the frequent transit

01:20:24.138 --> 01:20:28.714
network that provides some grid

01:20:28.842 --> 01:20:33.082
pattern that really helps benefit these new growth areas and

01:20:33.106 --> 01:20:36.522
then finally having that coverage to get in between some

01:20:36.546 --> 01:20:40.358
of those communities that fall into that network. So using

01:20:40.414 --> 01:20:44.390
this really real example that was presented here today, by providing that

01:20:44.430 --> 01:20:47.650
frequent transit network with some overlay with local services,

01:20:48.270 --> 01:20:52.006
there's confidence that you can connect to high frequency transit

01:20:52.038 --> 01:20:55.318
that can not only get you out of your community, but connect you to

01:20:55.374 --> 01:20:58.694
higher speed services to get you across our community. So that's really where you see

01:20:58.702 --> 01:21:02.518
the interplay between the different priorities that we've

01:21:02.534 --> 01:21:05.524
talked about here tonight. And several of the delegates have brought to our attention.

01:21:05.572 --> 01:21:09.188
Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you,

01:21:09.324 --> 01:21:12.516
Nathan. Hopefully you join the region of Waterloo paramedics

01:21:12.548 --> 01:21:16.356
in the future. Thanks for, thanks for taking the time tonight.

01:21:16.548 --> 01:21:20.948
Okay, we are now moving on to next delegation

01:21:21.044 --> 01:21:24.740
list. These are individuals who will have three minutes

01:21:24.820 --> 01:21:28.724
as you registered after the the timeline required for

01:21:28.732 --> 01:21:32.000
the five minute delegation. So I will call

01:21:32.040 --> 01:21:36.480
on Hiday Fizal and

01:21:36.520 --> 01:22:02.202
you have three minutes go

01:22:02.226 --> 01:22:05.390
to the podium. Howdy.

01:22:13.090 --> 01:22:16.554
Good evening to the chair and the rest of regional council.

01:22:16.722 --> 01:22:20.522
I'm here on behalf of Moore Transit Southern Ontario to support the

01:22:20.546 --> 01:22:24.516
GRT business plan. The GRT

01:22:24.548 --> 01:22:28.436
and business plan is an ambitious proposal to greatly improve

01:22:28.508 --> 01:22:32.244
transit across the Tri Cities and the rest of Waterloo region.

01:22:32.412 --> 01:22:36.020
We support the expansion of the frequent transit network

01:22:36.060 --> 01:22:39.732
from the existing two routes to 16 routes, additional mobility

01:22:39.796 --> 01:22:43.908
plus service hours, expanded transit service within small towns,

01:22:44.084 --> 01:22:47.972
free rides for kids under 12, which is something the GRT is

01:22:47.996 --> 01:22:51.324
behind on. Most, most agencies in Ontario

01:22:51.372 --> 01:22:55.292
already have this. And there's a lot of additional things in

01:22:55.316 --> 01:22:59.628
this plan that are excellent and we want to see the business plan

01:22:59.764 --> 01:23:02.972
funded and implemented as soon as possible. We also have

01:23:02.996 --> 01:23:06.444
some additional measures that we want to see to make the GRT

01:23:06.492 --> 01:23:10.188
service plan even better. The first one of this being fair

01:23:10.244 --> 01:23:13.452
capping, which is essentially a replacement of the

01:23:13.476 --> 01:23:17.292
monthly pass system to a maximum amount of fares someone can

01:23:17.316 --> 01:23:21.484
pay in a month. And what this does is it makes riders

01:23:21.612 --> 01:23:25.244
not need to calculate whether or not a monthly

01:23:25.292 --> 01:23:28.520
pass is cost effective for them to buy that month.

01:23:28.900 --> 01:23:32.268
So they can just tap and once they reach

01:23:32.324 --> 01:23:35.200
the monthly pass cost limit,

01:23:37.220 --> 01:23:40.604
they'll have free rides for the rest of the month. And another

01:23:40.692 --> 01:23:44.658
thing that I would want to see on the GRT

01:23:44.754 --> 01:23:47.954
is open credit and debit payment, which is also something the GRT

01:23:48.002 --> 01:23:51.666
is behind on. Most agencies within Ontario already

01:23:51.738 --> 01:23:55.490
have this, such as the ttc, Go Durham Region Transit and

01:23:55.530 --> 01:23:59.970
My Way. And we'd also like to see additional

01:24:00.130 --> 01:24:04.082
expansion of transit priority measures which the region is already doing a

01:24:04.106 --> 01:24:07.426
good job on, such as bus lanes, queue jumps and transit

01:24:07.458 --> 01:24:11.342
signal priority, as well as operational changes for more

01:24:11.366 --> 01:24:14.910
efficient services, particularly on buses such as

01:24:14.950 --> 01:24:18.286
proof of payment, a proof of payment

01:24:18.318 --> 01:24:21.774
system like seen on Ion, which would enable all door boarding on buses,

01:24:21.822 --> 01:24:25.598
speeding up boarding and speeding up trips. And another

01:24:25.654 --> 01:24:29.566
thing we realized was that The ION Stage 2 LRT wasn't included

01:24:29.598 --> 01:24:33.742
in this business plan. And we're concerned about the

01:24:33.766 --> 01:24:37.742
cost construction cost of this project and are worried that the

01:24:37.766 --> 01:24:41.022
high cost due to the lack of in house expertise

01:24:41.166 --> 01:24:44.446
and excessive use of contingencies

01:24:44.478 --> 01:24:47.822
and risk allocation for inflation are driving up

01:24:47.846 --> 01:24:51.582
the project and will lead to the project in November getting

01:24:51.686 --> 01:24:55.390
watered down to a bus lanes and improved

01:24:55.470 --> 01:24:59.086
priority measures on the 302 Ion bus and the LRT

01:24:59.118 --> 01:25:02.620
expansion to Cambridge and future expansions

01:25:03.680 --> 01:25:08.100
won't happen. So we want to see the GRT fund more

01:25:08.720 --> 01:25:12.216
staff to manage and build transit instead

01:25:12.248 --> 01:25:17.000
of relying on consultants which is proven to significantly

01:25:17.080 --> 01:25:20.860
increase construction costs to make this project financially viable.

01:25:21.440 --> 01:25:23.620
Thank you all for your time. Thank you.

01:25:24.480 --> 01:25:28.406
Commissioner Spooner, do you just want to respond to some of what Hadeel said?

01:25:28.488 --> 01:25:32.010
Yeah, Hattie, thanks for coming in. I was having a quick

01:25:32.050 --> 01:25:35.322
giggle there and looking at my team on the fair capping discussion. We are

01:25:35.346 --> 01:25:38.682
actively having that discussion as an organization. So we've got some

01:25:38.706 --> 01:25:42.550
technology limitations today, but absolutely exploring that

01:25:43.250 --> 01:25:46.826
on the second one on credit and debit. So on March 19th

01:25:46.858 --> 01:25:50.202
we launched GRT Pay. So we now do have the ability to

01:25:50.226 --> 01:25:53.034
accept debit and credit. We've got a single product on there now which is a

01:25:53.042 --> 01:25:57.040
single adult fare, but over time we'll continue to add to there. So that is

01:25:57.340 --> 01:26:01.012
our response to the need that you've identified for credit and debit

01:26:01.156 --> 01:26:04.240
in terms of priority measures in the bus network.

01:26:05.020 --> 01:26:08.692
Our opportunity for that is through individual projects that we will

01:26:08.716 --> 01:26:11.940
advance, but also through the development of the integrated mobility plan

01:26:11.980 --> 01:26:15.652
that looks at corridor usage in its entirety. So that plan will start

01:26:15.676 --> 01:26:19.204
to emerge in the coming year and we'll be able to have that discussion about

01:26:19.372 --> 01:26:23.072
where we need transit signal priority. As we often note,

01:26:23.136 --> 01:26:27.264
the success of ION stage one is that reliability and predictability piece.

01:26:27.392 --> 01:26:30.340
And then finally on stage two, lrt.

01:26:30.840 --> 01:26:34.592
So we do do things different in the region of Waterloo. We delivered a really

01:26:34.616 --> 01:26:38.528
high functioning LRT system at a really good price and

01:26:38.584 --> 01:26:42.032
so the goal on stage two is to figure out how

01:26:42.056 --> 01:26:45.264
to do that again. We've got a great in house team now managing,

01:26:45.392 --> 01:26:48.976
managing stage one and if we're able to leverage that to help reduce

01:26:49.008 --> 01:26:52.768
cost in, in stage two and eventually into stage three. We're looking at delivery

01:26:52.784 --> 01:26:55.984
models right now. So again all your feedback is great. Appreciate the

01:26:55.992 --> 01:26:59.616
opportunity to respond. Thank you. Thank you for coming in tonight.

01:26:59.808 --> 01:27:03.040
Next we have Abdullah, you are

01:27:03.080 --> 01:27:15.322
virtual I hi.

01:27:15.466 --> 01:27:18.794
Hello Councillors, chairmembers and members of

01:27:18.802 --> 01:27:22.330
the public. I would like to first of all

01:27:22.370 --> 01:27:23.350
show my support.

01:27:25.890 --> 01:27:27.910
First of all I would like to show my.

01:27:29.170 --> 01:27:32.522
Sorry about that camera. Yes, I would like to show my

01:27:32.546 --> 01:27:36.042
support to the business plan. I especially find

01:27:36.146 --> 01:27:40.090
the support, the frequent transit network and

01:27:40.130 --> 01:27:44.120
consistent schedules to be really good plans

01:27:44.620 --> 01:27:48.404
in improving the GRT system and I can surely explain

01:27:48.452 --> 01:27:52.292
why. It's because like when people drive a car you

01:27:52.316 --> 01:27:55.396
often have like, kind of like this assumption that you can just leave

01:27:55.428 --> 01:27:58.756
your house whenever you want and just go

01:27:58.908 --> 01:28:02.916
when you need to. But oftentimes with transit and I am also a frequent

01:28:02.948 --> 01:28:06.548
transit user by the way, I find that you have to really

01:28:06.604 --> 01:28:09.950
plan your day around transit and while this isn't really

01:28:09.990 --> 01:28:13.406
necessarily the end of world, it can really make the user

01:28:13.438 --> 01:28:17.502
experience for transit difficult for many people not being able to

01:28:17.606 --> 01:28:21.726
have flexibility and when you want to leave and constant scheduling.

01:28:21.918 --> 01:28:25.790
And so I think the. So of course when you have a 10

01:28:25.830 --> 01:28:29.182
minute frequency then you can, you don't really need to look at a

01:28:29.206 --> 01:28:32.990
schedule and you can leave and have

01:28:33.030 --> 01:28:37.212
a bus arrive within relative frequency.

01:28:37.276 --> 01:28:41.100
And I kind of experience that with the Ion. I take the Ion

01:28:41.260 --> 01:28:44.972
pretty regularly and also the CO2 bus

01:28:45.076 --> 01:28:48.716
fairly regularly and during peak times both

01:28:48.788 --> 01:28:52.160
transits have an approximately 10 minute

01:28:52.580 --> 01:28:56.092
frequency and that is a big experience

01:28:56.196 --> 01:29:00.028
in my, in my. That is a big improvement in my experience

01:29:00.164 --> 01:29:04.170
over the other local buses which often have a 30 minute

01:29:04.250 --> 01:29:06.150
frequency or even more.

01:29:07.170 --> 01:29:11.274
And using the ION and other buses that have ATAMF

01:29:11.322 --> 01:29:15.530
frequencies are just so much easy and so much more convenient.

01:29:15.690 --> 01:29:19.370
So I think that we should really go ahead with the

01:29:19.410 --> 01:29:23.002
plan to I think it's like 16

01:29:23.106 --> 01:29:26.746
routes or 15 routes that will have 10 minute

01:29:26.778 --> 01:29:29.908
or better transit frequency and

01:29:29.964 --> 01:29:33.796
also to have more and better frequency consistent schedules

01:29:33.908 --> 01:29:37.380
because if the schedule change a lot then that also kind of

01:29:37.420 --> 01:29:40.900
makes planning your trips more difficult.

01:29:40.980 --> 01:29:45.200
As well. And finally, I do have a question about

01:29:45.580 --> 01:29:48.324
the cost of this business plan. Like,

01:29:48.492 --> 01:29:52.404
how will it be funded? Because it does include a big expansion.

01:29:52.532 --> 01:29:55.548
And will fares go up? Will they stay the same

01:29:55.604 --> 01:29:59.116
and by how much, if that has been decided yet or not. Okay,

01:29:59.148 --> 01:30:02.492
thank you. But other than that, thank you very much. And I

01:30:02.516 --> 01:30:06.188
think this is a pretty good plan overall. Thank you. Thank you,

01:30:06.324 --> 01:30:09.676
Abdullah. I think Commissioner Spooner may be able to respond

01:30:09.708 --> 01:30:13.212
to some of the financial question, the last financial question you just

01:30:13.236 --> 01:30:16.492
had. Absolutely. Through your chair. And thank you

01:30:16.516 --> 01:30:19.948
for the opportunity. So, Abdullah, you rightly pointed

01:30:19.964 --> 01:30:23.644
out this is an investment plan. It takes our annual operating

01:30:23.692 --> 01:30:27.426
costs from 234 million to 314

01:30:27.498 --> 01:30:31.298
million. That is an operating cost increase of 34%.

01:30:31.434 --> 01:30:35.346
The piece that we're most excited about is that it also increases service hours

01:30:35.418 --> 01:30:39.282
by 63%. And this is because we've got a lot of the foundational

01:30:39.346 --> 01:30:43.250
elements for our transit system in place, and we're able to now really

01:30:43.290 --> 01:30:47.266
crank up that efficiency. It also anticipates that revenue

01:30:47.298 --> 01:30:51.468
will increase by 35% from its current 85 million to 115

01:30:51.524 --> 01:30:55.244
million. So we've talked a lot tonight about free transit. We haven't necessarily

01:30:55.292 --> 01:30:59.228
talked about how transit gets funded. That is in two ways. There is

01:30:59.364 --> 01:31:02.812
user pay, so that's at the farebox, that's the tap card or

01:31:02.836 --> 01:31:06.360
the money we put in. And then there's also community pay, which is where

01:31:06.740 --> 01:31:10.252
the tax base or the broader community bears some of that cost as

01:31:10.276 --> 01:31:13.772
well. And so in the fare strategy, we did identify that.

01:31:13.796 --> 01:31:17.544
We'll continue to look at like agencies. We'll look at an inflation,

01:31:17.672 --> 01:31:21.736
and we'll look at when we add major service increases.

01:31:21.848 --> 01:31:25.640
We may also recommend to council some fare increases, but really

01:31:25.680 --> 01:31:29.240
the goal is to maintain our cost recovery at its current rate, which is just

01:31:29.280 --> 01:31:33.272
hovering just under 40% while we grow. And so

01:31:33.456 --> 01:31:36.616
really, you know, how we fund it will happen in two ways.

01:31:36.648 --> 01:31:39.752
And my first one is encouragement to anybody listening tonight who's excited about

01:31:39.776 --> 01:31:42.568
this plan is come and ride with us, because that's the best way. If we've

01:31:42.584 --> 01:31:45.602
got ridership and revenue coming in, that's an easy choice.

01:31:45.666 --> 01:31:49.250
And the second one is that we will each year advance,

01:31:49.410 --> 01:31:52.418
as we've said, a transit service plan to regional council.

01:31:52.474 --> 01:31:56.146
And in that very difficult process of weighing all of the needs of the region,

01:31:56.298 --> 01:31:59.778
we'll obviously advocate for increased transit. So again,

01:31:59.834 --> 01:32:02.786
we've got a role in this as well, which is. Let's get on the bus.

01:32:02.858 --> 01:32:07.122
Thank you. Thank you. And thank you. For delegating

01:32:07.186 --> 01:32:10.082
tonight. Abdullah, I have no questions from council.

01:32:10.266 --> 01:32:14.082
We will move on to the next delegation. Swanee, Graham, I believe you

01:32:14.106 --> 01:32:16.790
are in person. You'll have three minutes.

01:32:28.010 --> 01:32:29.030
Okay.

01:32:33.210 --> 01:32:36.674
Good evening, everyone. I also have a question

01:32:36.762 --> 01:32:39.960
about free ridership for seniors. I myself am

01:32:40.000 --> 01:32:43.256
a senior and I think Brian from Supportive

01:32:43.288 --> 01:32:46.872
Housing answered a lot of questions and did a lot

01:32:46.896 --> 01:32:50.460
of the research, but I didn't know about that beforehand.

01:32:50.800 --> 01:32:53.672
But anyway, specifically,

01:32:53.816 --> 01:32:57.848
ridership for seniors, free. Between certain hours

01:32:57.904 --> 01:33:01.480
of the day when the ridership is really low and the buses are empty,

01:33:01.640 --> 01:33:05.292
it would really be a good idea to come up with some type of creative

01:33:05.356 --> 01:33:08.540
solution at any time

01:33:08.580 --> 01:33:11.308
during the day, like 10 to 2,

01:33:11.444 --> 01:33:15.292
to have seniors be able to just get on a bus and go where they

01:33:15.316 --> 01:33:18.924
have to go. Now, the cities, as Brian

01:33:18.972 --> 01:33:22.364
pointed out, of Burlington, Brampton, Oakville, Ottawa,

01:33:22.412 --> 01:33:25.948
Mississauga and a number of others, have already seen the benefits

01:33:26.044 --> 01:33:29.538
of free transit for seniors,

01:33:29.684 --> 01:33:32.966
including reduced traffic congestion,

01:33:33.158 --> 01:33:36.934
reduced social isolation, and according

01:33:37.022 --> 01:33:40.534
to the National Institute of aging, by 2030,

01:33:40.622 --> 01:33:44.118
so within the next five years, 25% of Canadians

01:33:44.214 --> 01:33:49.190
will be seniors. Now, I live in a building with 120

01:33:49.310 --> 01:33:53.030
apartments where at least 80% of

01:33:53.070 --> 01:33:55.764
the people are 65 years and older.

01:33:55.902 --> 01:33:59.980
And they come from a variety of financial backgrounds.

01:34:00.320 --> 01:34:04.040
They all have different stories. Now,

01:34:04.080 --> 01:34:07.480
many of these older seniors still drive,

01:34:07.640 --> 01:34:11.500
even though they absolutely shouldn't. Trust me, they shouldn't.

01:34:12.160 --> 01:34:16.152
And even though there is a bus stop directly in front

01:34:16.176 --> 01:34:19.608
of our building that goes right to the boardwalk and

01:34:19.664 --> 01:34:23.022
across the street that goes directly downtown. So they

01:34:23.046 --> 01:34:26.270
can certainly go to all of their doctor's appointments and to any

01:34:26.310 --> 01:34:29.726
appointments that they do have. However, most of them

01:34:29.798 --> 01:34:33.262
are still stubborn and have cars that they

01:34:33.286 --> 01:34:37.290
can drive to these appointments. And they drive because

01:34:38.230 --> 01:34:42.462
fees for transit is more expensive than putting

01:34:42.606 --> 01:34:46.290
a few dollars of gas in their car and going to their appointment.

01:34:46.870 --> 01:34:50.350
And these seniors have a lot of flexibility in

01:34:50.390 --> 01:34:54.330
terms of when they need transit. Like, they could go at any

01:34:54.370 --> 01:34:57.802
time of the day, not like students or people that

01:34:57.826 --> 01:35:01.306
go to jobs. And so they're totally flexible.

01:35:01.498 --> 01:35:04.586
But even if the senior can figure

01:35:04.618 --> 01:35:08.330
out how to apply for the affordable transit fare or

01:35:08.370 --> 01:35:11.386
the lower fare, it's still $48 a month.

01:35:11.458 --> 01:35:14.906
So let's say they go to a doctor once a week. That's 12

01:35:14.978 --> 01:35:16.070
bucks a pop.

01:35:17.970 --> 01:35:21.340
And also if they.

01:35:23.080 --> 01:35:26.992
Or if they Pay cash, it's $4 there. And if it's

01:35:27.056 --> 01:35:30.496
longer than 90 minutes, which is often the case for a senior,

01:35:30.688 --> 01:35:34.160
then it's going to be $8. And if they go with a partner,

01:35:34.240 --> 01:35:37.860
which is often the case, it's going to be twice that expensive.

01:35:38.200 --> 01:35:41.760
So putting gas in their car and driving to an appointment is an

01:35:41.800 --> 01:35:44.020
easier and cheaper solution.

01:35:44.520 --> 01:35:48.584
And most seniors, and I know this because I belong on

01:35:48.592 --> 01:35:51.912
the tenants association, most of these seniors

01:35:51.976 --> 01:35:55.656
are not likely to research online how to save

01:35:55.688 --> 01:35:58.408
a few bucks. Like, if they're going to save a lot of money,

01:35:58.464 --> 01:36:01.880
sure, everybody does. But for the amount of times that they

01:36:01.920 --> 01:36:04.500
travel, they're not going to do all of the research.

01:36:06.480 --> 01:36:10.948
And they're certainly not going to go with their walkers or

01:36:11.104 --> 01:36:14.252
without their walkers with a cane up to one of the

01:36:14.276 --> 01:36:17.532
kiosks and try to figure out how to use them.

01:36:17.636 --> 01:36:21.212
It just won't happen. If you could try and summarize. I've given you

01:36:21.236 --> 01:36:24.716
a little extra time just to summarize. And we have a question after. Thank you.

01:36:24.788 --> 01:36:28.060
Yep, these are all transportation barriers.

01:36:28.140 --> 01:36:31.692
And free, unfettered transit during low ridership

01:36:31.756 --> 01:36:35.388
hours is the best way to encourage more seniors

01:36:35.484 --> 01:36:38.970
to use public transit and get them off the roads.

01:36:39.470 --> 01:36:42.410
Thank you. We have a question from Chair Redmond.

01:36:43.150 --> 01:36:46.390
Thank you very much for coming. And I think you make a really interesting point

01:36:46.430 --> 01:36:49.974
how it's easier to put gas in your car. It is expensive to own a

01:36:49.982 --> 01:36:53.142
vehicle. It's expensive to run a vehicle. So I guess I

01:36:53.166 --> 01:36:56.262
would ask you, and maybe Commissioner Spooner, too, I don't know if there's been

01:36:56.286 --> 01:36:59.910
any research. Have you talked to people what would incentivize them for

01:36:59.950 --> 01:37:03.568
getting on the bus, just to be able to walk up to the bus

01:37:03.624 --> 01:37:07.120
stop and get on whenever they need it, as opposed to having the

01:37:07.160 --> 01:37:10.272
barriers of applying online, figuring all

01:37:10.296 --> 01:37:13.584
of that out. If somebody could just walk straight up to a bus and the

01:37:13.592 --> 01:37:17.008
bus is half empty or 90% empty,

01:37:17.184 --> 01:37:21.120
just to be able to walk up and put in two tunies. Now, of course,

01:37:21.240 --> 01:37:24.860
in a number of years, these seniors are going to be much more

01:37:25.160 --> 01:37:28.578
digitally adept, but at present, for the next

01:37:28.634 --> 01:37:32.210
so many years, they aren't. And so just being able

01:37:32.250 --> 01:37:35.870
to walk up, put in two Tunis and go wherever they have to go,

01:37:36.570 --> 01:37:40.786
I guess I asked Commissioner Spooner, have there been any studies about incentivizing?

01:37:40.898 --> 01:37:44.802
And it doesn't just have to be seniors, for that matter, but people to choose

01:37:44.866 --> 01:37:49.442
bus service over having a car make that transition through.

01:37:49.466 --> 01:37:53.234
You chair a massive amount of work in the industry related

01:37:53.322 --> 01:37:57.144
to, you know, demand management and moving

01:37:57.192 --> 01:38:00.936
people into different modes. I think you've picked up a few of the barriers and

01:38:01.008 --> 01:38:04.344
some of our previous speakers did the same, which is there's a car sitting in

01:38:04.352 --> 01:38:07.672
your driveway. It will take you 15ft from your front door to 15ft from your

01:38:07.696 --> 01:38:10.900
destination. And you don't require anything additional. You've already done the work.

01:38:11.840 --> 01:38:15.864
We offer a number of programs. We do travel training for

01:38:15.952 --> 01:38:19.262
people who are going to explore transit. In that program, we help them get their

01:38:19.336 --> 01:38:22.402
first transit pass, we help them ride their most common route. So we do have

01:38:22.426 --> 01:38:25.230
programs there. We've been talking about expanding that program,

01:38:26.330 --> 01:38:29.458
but you know, you've hit some really interesting barriers.

01:38:29.554 --> 01:38:33.666
I will remind you we moved with council support, the transfer window

01:38:33.698 --> 01:38:37.842
from 90 minutes to 120 minutes. So we do now have the opportunity for two

01:38:37.866 --> 01:38:41.474
hours. But some really good ideas here that I think we'll take away. And I

01:38:41.482 --> 01:38:45.106
just thank you for your delegation. Okay, thank you, thank you. Hold on one moment.

01:38:45.218 --> 01:38:48.582
Councillor Huneck, Chair. James, I think as

01:38:48.606 --> 01:38:52.726
a follow up to Chair Redmond's question, I'm just wondering from Commissioner Spinner,

01:38:52.758 --> 01:38:56.134
has there been any research into different

01:38:56.222 --> 01:38:59.782
fairs at different times of day, so not just for seniors, but for

01:38:59.806 --> 01:39:03.970
people in general if ridership is low between 10 and 2?

01:39:05.150 --> 01:39:09.062
Yeah, through you, Chair. And thank you, Councillor. So we've seen something really interesting

01:39:09.126 --> 01:39:12.438
happen on the back end of the pandemic. So we used to talk about AM

01:39:12.494 --> 01:39:16.166
and PM peak in transit. We don't talk about that anymore. It's actually completely

01:39:16.198 --> 01:39:19.670
flattened through the day. That's due to work

01:39:19.710 --> 01:39:23.330
from home. It's due to different travel patterns, a lot of flexibility. And so

01:39:24.350 --> 01:39:27.926
we've got data now that shows us that we have very small peaks

01:39:27.958 --> 01:39:31.558
now and that that stays consistent in the day. That being said, there is lots

01:39:31.574 --> 01:39:35.414
of research around how you could provide time of day transit to various

01:39:35.462 --> 01:39:39.216
groups. A challenge for us in that we are operating group fairs

01:39:39.248 --> 01:39:43.344
and so how to structure that. There is a technology limitation

01:39:43.392 --> 01:39:46.864
that we have in this program, but again with grtpay,

01:39:46.992 --> 01:39:50.592
a lot more flexible in our ability to deliver products. So again,

01:39:50.696 --> 01:39:53.552
some interesting stuff for us to chew on in this delegation.

01:39:53.696 --> 01:39:56.940
Just like using Hydro at certain points in the day.

01:39:57.720 --> 01:40:01.440
Thanks to the delegation, great ideas. Thank you. Thank you for coming

01:40:01.480 --> 01:40:04.740
in today. Next we have Madhav,

01:40:06.690 --> 01:40:09.150
